var e=JSON.parse('[[1901,"The difference between JavaScript and TypeScript is minimal on small, simple projects. If the codebase is small, rarely changes and is written by a single developer, typing offers few advantages. TypeScript also barely helps in code without complex data structures. In such cases it can be excessive. It all depends on the scale and life span of the project.","TypeScript is not a mandatory choice for every project.\\n\\nWhen TypeScript gives the least benefit\\nThe difference between JS and TS is barely noticeable if:\\n- The project is small\\n- a handful of files\\n- limited logic\\n- a short life cycle\\n\\n- There are no complex data types\\n- simple objects\\n- minimal nesting\\n- no complex API contracts\\n\\n- The code rarely changes\\n- there is no active development\\n- there is no frequent refactoring\\n\\nThe additional costs\\nEven when the benefit is minimal, TypeScript still requires:\\n- setting up the build\\n- configuring types\\n- maintaining the type descriptions\\n\\nWhen JS wins\\n\\n- quick prototypes\\n- experiments\\n- one-off scripts\\n\\nConclusion\\nTypeScript is most effective where there is complexity and growth. In simple scenarios JavaScript remains a rational choice.",null],[1902,"The function is typed with generic parameters and `keyof`. The key is constrained to the object\'s keys, and the type of the return value depends on the key that was passed. This lets TypeScript infer the type precisely and prevents access to non-existent properties. The approach makes code safe and self-documenting.","The task is to tie the type of the key to the type of the object\'s value.\\n\\nDefinition\\n\\n- `keyof T` — the union of all the keys of the object `T`.\\n- `T[K]` — the type of the value at the key `K`.\\n\\nBasic typing of the function\\n\\nAn example of use\\n\\nWhat this approach gives you\\n\\n- You cannot pass a non-existent key.\\n- The type of the result depends on the key.\\n- Autocompletion works in the IDE.\\n\\nWhy not just string\\n\\nThe downsides:\\n- There is no checking of keys.\\n- Types are lost.\\n- Runtime errors become possible.\\n\\nConclusion\\nUsing `keyof` together with generic parameters is the standard way of accessing an object\'s properties type-safely in TypeScript.","function getValue<T, K extends keyof T>(obj: T, key: K): T[K] {\\n  return obj[key];\\n}\\n\\nconst user = {\\n  id: 1,\\n  name: \\"Alex\\",\\n  active: true,\\n};\\n\\nconst name = getValue(user, \\"name\\");     // string\\nconst active = getValue(user, \\"active\\"); // boolean\\n\\nfunction bad(obj: any, key: string) {\\n  return obj[key];\\n}"],[1903,"To make TypeScript complain about a non-existent key you need to constrain the keys with `keyof` and avoid the `string` or `any` types. If the key is typed as `keyof T`, the compiler will only allow access to properties that really exist on the object. Any attempt to pass an invalid key will raise an error during type checking. This is the main mechanism for type-safe access to objects.","By default TypeScript can be too \\"lenient\\" if you give it a key type that is too general.\\n\\nDefinition\\n`keyof` is a TypeScript operator that forms the union of all the valid keys of an object.\\n\\nThe problem without `keyof`\\n\\nWhat is wrong\\n\\n- TypeScript does not know whether such a key exists.\\n- There is no error even for `get(obj, \\"b\\")`.\\n- The type of the result becomes unsafe (`any` or `unknown`).\\n\\nThe correct approach with `keyof`\\n\\nThe behaviour\\n\\nUse with a concrete type\\n\\nAdditional strictness measures\\n\\n- Enable `noImplicitAny`.\\n- Enable `strict`.\\n- Avoid index signatures unless they are necessary.\\n\\nConclusion\\nTo guarantee that TypeScript complains about non-existent keys, the key must always be typed through `keyof` rather than as an arbitrary string.","function get(obj: { a: number }, key: string) {\\n  return obj[key];\\n}\\n\\nfunction get<T, K extends keyof T>(obj: T, key: K): T[K] {\\n  return obj[key];\\n}\\n\\nconst user = { id: 1, name: \\"Alex\\" };\\n\\nget(user, \\"id\\");    // ok\\nget(user, \\"name\\");  // ok\\nget(user, \\"age\\");   // TypeScript error\\n\\ntype User = {\\n  id: number;\\n  name: string;\\n};\\n\\nfunction getUserValue(user: User, key: keyof User) {\\n  return user[key];\\n}\\n\\n// weakens the checking\\ntype Bad = {\\n  [key: string]: string;\\n};"],[1904,"A generic is constrained to an object\'s keys with the construct `K extends keyof T`. This guarantees that `K` can only be one of the keys of the object `T`. The approach lets you tie the type of the key to the type of the return value and get precise typing. It is the basic and most widespread pattern in TypeScript.","Constraining a generic is a way of telling the compiler which values are allowed.\\n\\nDefinition\\nA generic constraint is a restriction on a type parameter expressed with `extends`.\\n\\nA basic example\\n\\nWhat is happening here\\n\\n- `T` — the object type.\\n- `K` — the key type.\\n- `K extends keyof T` — the key can only be an existing one.\\n- `T[K]` — the type of the value depends on the key.\\n\\nAn example of use\\n\\nWhy this is needed\\n\\n- Protection against typos.\\n- Correct types without `as`.\\n- Better autocompletion.\\n- Safe universal utilities.\\n\\nAn extended example: writing a value\\n\\nThe behaviour\\n\\nConclusion\\nConstraining a generic with `K extends keyof T` is a fundamental TypeScript technique for building strict, safe APIs when working with objects.","function getValue<T, K extends keyof T>(obj: T, key: K): T[K] {\\n  return obj[key];\\n}\\n\\nconst settings = {\\n  theme: \\"dark\\",\\n  retry: 3,\\n};\\n\\ngetValue(settings, \\"theme\\"); // string\\ngetValue(settings, \\"retry\\"); // number\\ngetValue(settings, \\"url\\");   // error\\n\\nfunction setValue<T, K extends keyof T>(\\n  obj: T,\\n  key: K,\\n  value: T[K]\\n) {\\n  obj[key] = value;\\n}\\n\\nsetValue(settings, \\"retry\\", 5);     // ok\\nsetValue(settings, \\"retry\\", \\"5\\");   // error"],[1905,"TypeScript gives you autocompletion, compile-time checks and safe refactoring, none of which plain CSS has. Errors in the names of tokens, colours or variants are caught before the application runs. This is especially convenient in large projects and design systems. The result is styles that are more predictable and maintainable.","Plain CSS is flexible but offers almost no protection against errors.\\n\\nDefinition\\nDefinition: typing in TS is a way of restricting the allowed values and data structures in advance, so that errors are found during development rather than in the browser.\\n\\nWhat TypeScript adds on top of CSS\\n\\n- Autocompletion\\n- the IDE suggests the available tokens, variants and themes.\\n\\n- Error checking\\n- typos in names (`primaryy`) are caught immediately.\\n\\n- Safe refactoring\\n- renaming a token updates every use of it.\\n\\n- Restricting values\\n- you can state the allowed variants explicitly (`\\"small\\" | \\"medium\\" | \\"large\\"`).\\n\\nExample: constraints through TS\\n\\nIn plain CSS such errors would only show up visually.\\n\\nWhere this is especially useful\\n\\n- Design systems and UI kits\\n- Theming (light/dark, brand themes)\\n- Large teams and long-lived projects\\n\\nConclusion\\nTyping makes styles safer, more convenient and more scalable, especially once CSS stops being \\"just a couple of files\\" and becomes part of the architecture.","type Size = \\"sm\\" | \\"md\\" | \\"lg\\";\\n\\nexport const button = {\\n  sm: \\"4px\\",\\n  md: \\"8px\\",\\n  lg: \\"12px\\",\\n} satisfies Record<Size, string>;"],[1906,"The main difficulties arise with generics, complex union types and type inference. Sometimes TS requires more code than you would like in order to express your intent. It can also be hard to type dynamic structures and third-party libraries. Over time these problems get solved with patterns and practice.","TypeScript is powerful but not always obvious.\\n\\nCommon difficulties\\n\\n1. Generics\\n\\n- Signatures are hard to read\\n- Errors look unwieldy\\n- It is not always obvious where `<T>` is needed\\n\\n2. Union types and narrowing\\n\\n- You have to narrow the type explicitly\\n- Without checks TS does not \\"trust\\" the developer\\n\\n3. Type inference\\n\\n- Sometimes TS infers too wide a type\\n- You have to help it with `as const` or explicit annotations\\n\\n4. Third-party libraries\\n\\n- The types may be inaccurate\\n- Sometimes you have to write a `d.ts`\\n- The versions of the types and of the library can drift apart\\n\\nHow people usually cope with this\\n\\n- They break types down into simpler ones\\n- They use utility types (`Pick`, `Omit`)\\n- They are not afraid of explicit annotations\\n\\nConclusion\\nDifficulties in TypeScript are a normal part of the work. The main thing is to understand why the compiler is complaining and to be able to translate business logic into comprehensible types.","function identity<T>(value: T): T {\\n  return value;\\n}\\n\\ntype Result = { ok: true; data: string } | { ok: false; error: Error };\\n\\nif (result.ok) {\\n  // data is available\\n}"],[1907,"Declarative merging of interfaces is TypeScript\'s ability to combine several declarations of the same interface into one. All the fields from the different declarations are gathered together automatically. This is used to extend types without rewriting them. It is most often applied when working with libraries and global types.","Definition\\nDeclaration merging is a TypeScript mechanism by which several declarations with the same name are combined into a single type.\\n\\nAn example with an interface\\n\\nThe result:\\n- `User` has the fields `id` and `name`\\n\\nWhere this is used\\n\\n- Extending the types of third-party libraries\\n- Adding fields to global interfaces\\n- Describing an API that is \\"grown\\" piece by piece\\n\\nLimitations\\n\\n- It works only with `interface`\\n- `type` cannot be merged this way\\n- Conflicting types will produce an error\\n\\nAn example of practical use\\n\\nConclusion\\nDeclarative merging of interfaces lets you extend types cleanly without changing the original source code, and it is an important part of the TypeScript ecosystem.","interface User {\\n  id: number;\\n}\\n\\ninterface User {\\n  name: string;\\n}\\n\\ninterface Window {\\n  myAppVersion: string;\\n}"],[1908,"`Omit` is a utility type that lets you create a new type by excluding one or more fields from an existing one. It does not change the original type but returns a new one. It is most often used when you need to pass an object without internal or sensitive fields. This makes types cleaner and safer.","Definition\\n`Omit` is a built-in TypeScript utility type that creates the type `T` without the properties `K`.\\n\\nA basic example\\n\\n`PublicUser` contains:\\n- `id`\\n- `name`\\n\\nRemoving several fields\\n\\nWhere this is used\\n\\n- DTOs for an API\\n- Edit forms\\n- Public data models\\n- Removing internal fields (`createdAt`, `updatedAt`)\\n\\nAn important nuance\\n`Omit`:\\n- works only at the type level\\n- does not remove the field at runtime\\n\\nConclusion\\n`Omit` is a convenient, safe way of getting a derived type without the unneeded fields, with no need to duplicate the description of the structure.","type User = {\\n  id: number;\\n  name: string;\\n  password: string;\\n};\\n\\ntype PublicUser = Omit<User, \'password\'>;\\n\\ntype SafeUser = Omit<User, \'password\' | \'id\'>;"],[1909,"`Pick` lets you create a new type that includes only the selected fields from an existing one. It is the opposite of `Omit`. It is useful when you need a \\"narrow\\" type for a specific scenario. The original type is not changed in the process.","Definition\\n`Pick` is a utility type that forms a type from `T` containing only the properties `K`.\\n\\nExample\\n\\nPractical cases\\n\\n- Lists and previews\\n- Partial API responses\\n- Optimizing component props\\n- Explicitly restricting the available data\\n\\n`Pick` vs duplication\\n\\n- there is no need to describe the fields again\\n- fewer discrepancies when the base type changes\\n- better maintainability\\n\\nConclusion\\n`Pick` helps you express explicitly which part of the data is really needed, and makes types more readable and more resilient to change.","type User = {\\n  id: number;\\n  name: string;\\n  email: string;\\n};\\n\\ntype UserPreview = Pick<User, \'id\' | \'name\'>;"],[1910,"A Type Guard is a check that lets TypeScript determine the exact type of a value in a particular branch of code. It is used when working with union types, where the behaviour depends on the real type of the data. A Type Guard makes code safer and spares you unnecessary checks and type casts. It can be built in or custom.","Definition\\nA Type Guard is an expression or function that narrows the type of a value within a particular scope.\\n\\nBuilt-in Type Guards\\n\\n- `typeof`\\n- `instanceof`\\n- Checking for a property\\n\\nExample:\\n\\nA custom Type Guard\\n\\nUse:\\n\\nWhy they are needed\\n\\n- Safe work with union types\\n- Readable code without `as`\\n- Protection against runtime errors\\n\\nConclusion\\nA Type Guard is TypeScript\'s key tool for safe logic when the type of a value is determined at runtime.","function print(value: string | number) {\\n  if (typeof value === \'string\') {\\n    value.toUpperCase();\\n  }\\n}\\n\\ntype User = { name: string };\\ntype Admin = { name: string; role: string };\\n\\nfunction isAdmin(user: User | Admin): user is Admin {\\n  return \'role\' in user;\\n}\\n\\nif (isAdmin(u)) {\\n  u.role;\\n}"],[1911,"Interfaces in TypeScript let you define the structure of your data, providing typing for objects and making code easier to maintain. They give you strict type checking and help guarantee that data conforms to the expected shape.","In TypeScript interfaces are a powerful tool for ensuring data is strictly typed. Using interfaces helps guarantee that objects and data structures have the correct shape.\\n- Defining the structure of an object:\\n- An interface lets you specify the structure of an object, stating which fields are required and which are optional.\\n- Example:\\n\\n- Using interfaces for arrays and collections:\\n- Interfaces can also describe the types of the elements of arrays and collections.\\n- Example:\\n\\n- Interfaces for functions:\\n- Interfaces can be used to describe the structure of a function, stating the types of its parameters and its return value.\\n- Example:\\n\\nConclusion: interfaces in TypeScript help ensure data is strictly typed and improve the structure of your code, making it clearer and less error-prone.","interface User {\\n  name: string;\\n  age: number;\\n  email?: string; // an optional field\\n}\\n\\nconst user: User = { name: \'Alice\', age: 25 };\\n\\ninterface Product {\\n  id: number;\\n  name: string;\\n}\\n\\nconst products: Product[] = [\\n  { id: 1, name: \'Laptop\' },\\n  { id: 2, name: \'Phone\' }\\n];\\n\\ninterface SumFunction {\\n  (a: number, b: number): number;\\n}\\n\\nconst sum: SumFunction = (a, b) => a + b;"],[1912,"The main difficulty is that TypeScript does not control data at runtime, and a server can send back anything. Types describe the client\'s expectations but do not guarantee the actual structure of the response. Data is often incomplete, contains `null`, has extra fields or differs from the documentation. This leads to runtime errors despite a correct compilation. That is why typing server data requires additional checks.","TypeScript works only at compile time and cannot check the real data that arrives over the network. Because of this, several typical problems come up when working with a server.\\n\\nThe main difficulties of typing external data\\n\\n- No guarantees about the structure. The server may return an object that does not match the expected interface. TypeScript meanwhile considers the data valid if it has been cast to the required type.\\n- Mismatched data types\\n- a `number` may arrive as a string\\n- a required field may be missing\\n- a value may be `null` instead of an object\\n\\n- API evolution. Server contracts change over time, while the client\'s types can go stale. This is especially critical when there is no strict synchronization between teams.\\n- Complex nested structures. The deeper the nesting, the higher the chance of discrepancies and the harder the types are to maintain.\\n\\nAn example of the problem\\n\\nConclusion\\nTyping server data in TypeScript describes the client\'s expectations but does not guarantee that the data is correct. Additional runtime checks are necessary for reliability.","type User = {\\n  id: number\\n  name: string\\n}\\n\\nconst user = response as User\\n// TypeScript is happy, but the server may not have sent name"],[1913,"The problem is solved through explicit validation of the data at runtime. Validation schemas or manual checks are normally used. TypeScript types are supplemented with runtime checks to make sure the data matches expectations. Shared contracts between server and client also help. This reduces the number of hidden errors.","A mismatch between types and real data cannot be solved by TypeScript alone. Additional approaches are used for this.\\n\\nThe main approaches\\n\\n- Runtime validation of the data. The data is checked as soon as it is received from the server. If the structure does not match, an error is thrown or fallback logic is applied.\\n- Explicit transformation of the data. The server\'s response is converted into the required format by hand. This lets you control `null`, default values and types.\\n- Contracts between server and client. These are used:\\n- OpenAPI\\n- GraphQL\\n- shared types in a monorepo\\n\\n- Isolating the API layer. Working with raw data is moved into a separate layer, and only already-validated structures reach the rest of the code.\\n\\nAn example of the approach\\n\\nConclusion\\nTypeScript types must be supplemented with runtime checks. Only a combination of compile-time and runtime approaches gives real reliability.","function parseUser(data: any): User {\\n  if (typeof data.id !== \'number\') {\\n    throw new Error(\'Invalid user id\')\\n  }\\n\\n  return {\\n    id: data.id,\\n    name: String(data.name ?? \'\')\\n  }\\n}"],[1914,"Technically you can make every field optional, but it degrades the quality of your types. The code stops protecting you from errors, because any field may be `undefined`. This leads to a large number of checks and complicates the logic. In the end TypeScript loses its value. This approach is considered an anti-pattern.","Using optional fields looks like a simple solution to the problems with server data, but in practice it creates new difficulties.\\n\\nWhat happens when optional fields are used en masse\\n\\n- Loss of strict typing. The type no longer describes real expectations, only possible variants.\\n- A growing number of checks in the code. In almost every place you have to write checks for `undefined`.\\n- Hidden errors. The logic can break silently if a field is unexpectedly missing.\\n- Reduced readability. It becomes unclear which fields are actually required for a component or function to work.\\n\\nAn example of the problem\\n\\nThe recommended approach\\n\\n- Make the fields without which the code does not work required\\n- Handle undefined values at the API boundary\\n- Use different types for \\"raw\\" and \\"normalized\\" data\\n\\nConclusion\\nOptional fields are acceptable, but using them en masse destroys the benefit of TypeScript. It is better to validate the data explicitly and work with guaranteed structures.","type User = {\\n  id?: number\\n  name?: string\\n}\\n\\n// you have to keep checking\\nif (!user.id) {\\n  return\\n}"],[1915,"TypeScript helps you find errors before the code runs. It adds strict typing on top of JavaScript. Types improve the readability and predictability of code. This matters especially in large, team-based projects.","TypeScript solves a number of problems that are difficult or impossible to catch in plain JavaScript while the code is being written.\\n\\nDefinition\\nTypeScript is a layer on top of JavaScript that adds static typing and compile-time checks.\\n\\nThe main problems TypeScript solves\\nIn practice TypeScript is used for the following purposes:\\n- Early error detection\\n- Type mismatches\\n- Incorrect function arguments\\n- Accessing properties that do not exist\\n\\n- Increasing code reliability\\n- Explicit contracts between parts of the application\\n- Fewer hidden runtime errors\\n\\n- Improving readability\\n- Types act as documentation\\n- It is easier to understand which data is expected and returned\\n\\n- Supporting scaling\\n- Code is easier to refactor\\n- It is easier to work as a team\\n\\nExample\\n\\nConclusion\\nTypeScript lets you move some errors from runtime to compile time. This makes development more predictable and safer, especially on large projects.","function sum(a: number, b: number): number {\\n  return a + b;\\n}"],[1916,"Code generation from an API specification is the automatic creation of types, clients and requests based on OpenAPI or Swagger. It reduces the amount of hand-written code and lowers the risk of errors. OpenAPI Generator and Swagger Codegen are often used.","Code generation is the process of creating client code from a description of an API.\\n\\nWhat an API specification is\\nDefinition: an API specification is a formal description of the server\'s endpoints, parameters and responses.\\n\\nMost often these are used:\\n- OpenAPI\\n- Swagger\\n\\nWhat can be generated\\n\\n- TypeScript types\\n- HTTP clients\\n- request functions\\n\\nAn example of the result\\n\\nThe advantages\\n\\n- Frontend and backend stay in sync\\n- Fewer errors\\n- Faster development\\n\\nWhere it is applied\\n\\n- Large projects\\n- Microservice architectures\\n- Teams with a separate backend\\n\\nConclusion\\nCode generation is especially useful when an API changes often and strict typing is required.","export interface User {\\n  id: number;\\n  name: string;\\n}"],[1917,"Utility types are built-in types for transforming other types. The ones used most often are `Partial`, `Required`, `Pick`, `Omit`, `Record` and `Readonly`. They help you create new types quickly without duplicating code.","Utility types let you modify existing types and create derived types.\\n\\nThe main utility types\\n\\n- Partial. Makes all fields optional.\\n\\n- Required. Makes all fields required.\\n- Pick. Selects specific fields.\\n\\n- Omit. Excludes fields.\\n\\n- Record. Creates a dictionary type.\\n\\n- Readonly. Makes fields immutable.\\n\\nWhere they are applied\\n\\n- API responses\\n- forms\\n- DTOs\\n\\nConclusion\\nUtility types speed up work with types considerably and let you avoid duplication.","type User = { name: string; age: number };\\ntype UpdateUser = Partial<User>;\\n\\ntype UserName = Pick<User, \\"name\\">;\\n\\ntype UserWithoutAge = Omit<User, \\"age\\">;\\n\\ntype Users = Record<string, User>;"],[1918,"Enums increase bundle size, because they turn into an object in JavaScript. They are less flexible than union types and sometimes complicate typing. There are also unexpected quirks when working with numeric enums and reverse mapping. That is why modern TypeScript often prefers string union types.","Enums in TypeScript are convenient but have a number of practical limitations.\\n\\n1) How an enum compiles\\nDefinition: an enum is a type that compiles into a JavaScript object containing the values and sometimes a reverse mapping. Example:\\n\\nCompilation results in an object being created.\\n\\n2) Increased bundle size\\nThe reason:\\n- An enum creates an object.\\n- A reverse mapping is sometimes added.\\n- On large projects this increases the resulting code.\\n\\n3) Less convenient typing\\nInstead of an enum people often use:\\n\\nThe advantages of a union:\\n- No extra code in JS.\\n- Easier to integrate with an API.\\n\\n4) Problems with numeric enums\\nNumeric enums:\\n- Allow non-obvious values.\\n- Can lead to errors in comparisons.\\n\\nExample:\\n\\n5) When an enum is justified after all\\n\\n- When you need a set of constants.\\n- When an explicit namespace matters.\\n- In library code.\\n\\nConclusion: enums are convenient but can increase bundle size and are less flexible than union types, so they are applied deliberately.","enum Status {\\n  Active,\\n  Disabled\\n}\\n\\ntype Status = \\"active\\" | \\"disabled\\";\\n\\nenum Role {\\n  Admin = 1\\n}\\n\\nlet r: Role = 999 as Role; // possible"],[1919,"The type of the `store` can be obtained by taking the return type of the function that creates it. `ReturnType` is used for this. The approach spares you from duplicating types by hand. It adapts to changes in the implementation automatically. This increases the reliability of your typing and reduces the number of errors.","In TypeScript it is important to aim at inferring types from the code rather than describing them by hand.\\n\\nDefinition\\nDefinition: ReturnType<T> is a TypeScript utility type that extracts the return type of the function `T`.\\n\\nAn example with a Redux store\\n\\nWhy do it this way\\n\\n- A single source of truth for types\\n- The type updates automatically\\n- Duplication is reduced\\n\\nWhere it is applied\\n\\n- Redux store\\n- Configurations\\n- Object factories\\n- API clients\\n\\nTypical mistakes\\n\\n- Describing complex types by hand\\n- Using `any` instead of inferring types\\n\\nConclusion\\n`ReturnType` lets you obtain precise types straight from functions, making your code resilient to change.","export const createStore = () => {\\n  return {\\n    getState: () => ({ user: null }),\\n    dispatch: (action: any) => {}\\n  };\\n};\\n\\nexport type Store = ReturnType<typeof createStore>;"],[1920,"`Omit` is used to remove properties from an object type. `Exclude` is used to exclude members from a union type. Despite their similar names, they work on different kinds of thing. `Omit` is usually applied to DTOs and component props. `Exclude` is for narrowing sets of possible values.","To apply `Omit` and `Exclude` correctly it is important to understand which level each type works on: the structure of an object, or a union of types.\\n\\nDefinition\\nDefinition: Omit is a utility type that removes the given keys from an object type. Exclude is a utility type that excludes members from a union type.\\n\\nOmit: working with objects\\n`Omit` is applied when you need to take an existing type and remove one or more fields from it.\\n\\nExample\\n\\nWhere it is used\\n\\n- The props of React components\\n- DTOs for an API\\n- Public representations of entities\\n\\nExclude: working with unions\\n`Exclude` is used to remove individual variants from a union.\\n\\nExample\\n\\nWhere it is used\\n\\n- Statuses\\n- Event types\\n- Restricting the allowed values\\n\\nA common mistake\\n\\n- Trying to apply `Exclude` to an object type\\n- Using `Omit` to work with a union\\n\\nConclusion\\n`Omit` works with the keys of objects, while `Exclude` works with the members of a union type; the choice depends on the shape of the source type.","type User = {\\n  id: string;\\n  email: string;\\n  password: string;\\n};\\n\\ntype PublicUser = Omit<User, \\"password\\">;\\n\\ntype Status = \\"loading\\" | \\"success\\" | \\"error\\";\\ntype StableStatus = Exclude<Status, \\"loading\\">;"],[1921,"The return type of a function can be obtained with `ReturnType` combined with `typeof`. If the function is asynchronous, `Awaited` is used in addition. This approach lets you reproduce a third-party library\'s types exactly. It protects you from drifting out of sync on updates. It is a basic practice when working with external SDKs and APIs.","When working with third-party libraries it is important not to rewrite their types by hand but to extract them straight from the declarations.\\n\\nDefinition\\nDefinition: typeof + ReturnType is a combination that lets you obtain the return type of a function from its declaration.\\n\\nA basic synchronous example\\n\\nThe asynchronous case\\nIf the function returns a `Promise`, the type needs to be \\"unwrapped\\".\\n\\nExample\\n\\nWhy this is needed\\n\\n- No duplication of types\\n- Changes are picked up automatically\\n- Precise typing of complex structures\\n\\nTypical scenarios\\n\\n- API clients\\n- SDKs of third-party services\\n- Hooks and factories\\n\\nTypical mistakes\\n\\n- Describing return types by hand\\n- Using `any` instead of inferring types\\n- Ignoring `Awaited` for async functions\\n\\nConclusion\\nThe combination of `typeof`, `ReturnType` and `Awaited` is the standard way of working safely with the types of third-party libraries.","import { createClient } from \\"some-lib\\";\\n\\ntype Client = ReturnType<typeof createClient>;\\n\\nimport { fetchUser } from \\"api-lib\\";\\n\\ntype User = Awaited<ReturnType<typeof fetchUser>>;"],[1922,"The setTimeout and setInterval functions are not part of the JavaScript language standard (ECMAScript). They are provided by the runtime environment, such as the browser or Node.js, through the Web API or other environment APIs. The ECMAScript specification contains no description of these functions. They are used for deferred or periodic execution of code, but their implementation and availability depend on the specific platform.","Which standard they belong to\\nThe `setTimeout` and `setInterval` functions are not part of the ECMAScript specification (the core JavaScript standard). They are defined in the environment\'s API:\\n- In browsers: part of the Web API standardized by the WHATWG and the W3C (for example, in the HTML Living Standard specification).\\n- In Node.js: part of the global object provided by the runtime.\\n\\nWhy this matters\\nSince these functions are not part of the language core, their behaviour and availability can differ between environments. For example, the minimum delay, the timer\'s accuracy or additional parameters may depend on the implementation.\\n\\nAn example of use\\n\\nConclusion\\n`setTimeout` and `setInterval` are widely available functions that are nevertheless not standardized in ECMAScript. They belong to the environment\'s API, which means they depend on the platform (browser, server and so on). Developers should keep this in mind when writing code for different runtimes.","// An example in the browser or Node.js\\nconsole.log(\'Start\');\\nsetTimeout(() => {\\n  console.log(\'Executed after 1 second\');\\n}, 1000);\\n\\nlet counter = 0;\\nconst intervalId = setInterval(() => {\\n  counter++;\\n  console.log(`Interval tick ${counter}`);\\n  if (counter >= 3) {\\n    clearInterval(intervalId);\\n    console.log(\'Interval stopped\');\\n  }\\n}, 500);"],[1923,"The \'Decorator\' pattern lets you add new behaviour to objects dynamically without changing their source code. It wraps the original object in a decorator object that has the same interface and delegates calls to the wrapped object, adding its own logic before or after. This is useful when you need to extend a class\'s functionality but inheritance is not suitable because of an explosion of subclasses. The decorator follows the open/closed principle: classes are open for extension but closed for modification.","The \'Decorator\' pattern is a structural design pattern that lets you add new functionality to objects dynamically by wrapping them in special decorator objects. Instead of changing the original class (which violates the open/closed principle), you create a chain of wrappers, each of which adds its own behaviour.\\n\\nHow it works\\nA decorator implements the same interface as the object it wraps. When a method is called, the decorator performs additional logic (before or after) and then delegates the call to the wrapped object. This lets you combine behaviour on the fly, creating flexible configurations.\\n\\nAn example in TypeScript\\n\\nWhere it is applied\\n\\n- In UI libraries, to add styles or behaviour to components (border, padding, for example).\\n- In streaming APIs (Java I/O, .NET Streams), to add filters (buffering, compression).\\n- In the middleware of web frameworks (Express.js, NestJS), to add logging and authentication.\\n\\nConclusion: use the \'Decorator\' pattern when you need to add responsibilities to objects flexibly at runtime, avoiding a rigid inheritance hierarchy and keeping the code clean and extensible.","// The base component interface\\ninterface Coffee {\\n    cost(): number;\\n    description(): string;\\n}\\n\\n// A concrete component\\nclass SimpleCoffee implements Coffee {\\n    cost() { return 5; }\\n    description() { return \'Simple coffee\'; }\\n}\\n\\n// The base decorator\\nabstract class CoffeeDecorator implements Coffee {\\n    constructor(protected coffee: Coffee) {}\\n    cost() { return this.coffee.cost(); }\\n    description() { return this.coffee.description(); }\\n}\\n\\n// Concrete decorators\\nclass MilkDecorator extends CoffeeDecorator {\\n    cost() { return super.cost() + 2; }\\n    description() { return super.description() + \', milk\'; }\\n}\\n\\nclass SugarDecorator extends CoffeeDecorator {\\n    cost() { return super.cost() + 1; }\\n    description() { return super.description() + \', sugar\'; }\\n}\\n\\n// Use\\nlet myCoffee: Coffee = new SimpleCoffee();\\nmyCoffee = new MilkDecorator(myCoffee);\\nmyCoffee = new SugarDecorator(myCoffee);\\nconsole.log(myCoffee.description()); // Simple coffee, milk, sugar\\nconsole.log(myCoffee.cost()); // 8"],[1924,"Generics in collections let you create data structures (lists, dictionaries, sets) that work with a particular type of element. This provides compile-time type checking, preventing errors when objects of an unsuitable type are added. For example, a List can contain only strings. This approach makes code safer and cleaner by removing the need for type casts.","Generics in collections are a mechanism that lets you parameterize collection classes with the concrete data type they will work with. This means that instead of using collections in the style of \\"raw\\" types, which can store any objects, you create, for example, a list intended exclusively for strings or numbers.\\n\\nHow it is implemented\\nAt the language level the compiler uses the technique of type erasure, or of preserving types, depending on the platform. In Java and C# generics provide compile-time type checking, but the type information is erased at runtime (in Java). In TypeScript types exist only at compile time in JavaScript.\\n\\nCode examples\\nHere is what it looks like in practice in different languages:\\n\\nWhere and how it is applied\\nGenerics in collections are used everywhere in modern development to:\\n- Ensure type safety: the compiler catches type-mismatch errors before the program runs.\\n- Remove the need for manual type casts, which makes code cleaner and faster.\\n- Create reusable, flexible components: one collection implementation can work with any type.\\n- Improve the readability and maintainability of code: from the signature it is immediately clear which data the collection works with.\\n\\nConclusion: using generics in collections is a mandatory practice for writing safe, maintainable and expressive code. They are worth applying whenever you need a data structure for storing elements of one particular type, in order to avoid runtime errors and explicit casts.","// Java: safe work with a list of strings\\nList<String> names = new ArrayList<>();\\nnames.add(\\"Alice\\");\\n// names.add(42); // Compile error: incompatible types\\nString first = names.get(0); // No cast required\\n\\n// C#: using a generic dictionary\\nDictionary<int, string> users = new Dictionary<int, string>();\\nusers.Add(1, \\"Bob\\");\\n// users.Add(\\"key\\", \\"value\\"); // Compile error\\nstring userName = users[1]; // Direct access with a typed key\\n\\n// TypeScript: a typed array\\nconst numbers: Array<number> = [1, 2, 3];\\nnumbers.push(4);\\n// numbers.push(\\"five\\"); // Compile error\\nconst doubled = numbers.map(n => n * 2); // The type of n is inferred as number"],[1925,"Type safety is a property of a programming language that guarantees operations are only performed on data of permissible types. It prevents errors such as trying to add a number to a string. In languages with strict static typing (TypeScript or Java, for example) these checks happen at compile time, which helps find bugs earlier. In dynamic languages (JavaScript, for example) many checks are deferred until the program runs, which can lead to runtime errors.","Type safety is a fundamental concept in programming which means that the language will not let you perform an operation on a value if that operation is not defined for its data type. The main goal is to prevent a whole class of errors that arise from unexpected behaviour of data — for example, trying to call a method that does not exist on an object, or performing an arithmetic operation on a string.\\n\\nHow it works\\nThe language\'s type system controls the matching of types. This can be done at different stages:\\n- Static type checking: the analysis happens before the program runs, at compile or lint time. Examples: TypeScript, Java, C#.\\n- Dynamic type checking: the checks are performed while the program runs. Examples: Python, JavaScript, Ruby.\\n\\nStatically typed languages often provide a higher level of type safety, because many errors are caught before the code even runs.\\n\\nA practical example\\nConsider the difference between JavaScript (dynamic typing) and TypeScript (static typing).\\n\\nWhere it is applied and why it is needed\\nType safety is critically important in large projects and teams, because it:\\n- Serves as a form of documentation, making function contracts explicit.\\n- Lets tools (IDEs) provide intelligent autocompletion and code navigation.\\n- Significantly reduces the number of errors caused by typos or incorrect API use, which are hard to catch in dynamic languages.\\n- Makes refactoring easier — the compiler helps you find every place that needs updating.\\n\\nConclusion: type safety is a powerful mechanism for increasing the reliability and maintainability of code. It is worth applying in any project where stability matters, especially when working in a team or maintaining an application long term. Using languages or layers with static typing (TypeScript for the frontend, languages like Go or Rust for the backend) is an investment in code quality that pays for itself in reduced debugging time.","// JavaScript (no checking as you write)\\nfunction calculateTotal(price, quantity) {\\n    return price * quantity;\\n}\\n// This will only cause an error at runtime if a string is passed\\nconsole.log(calculateTotal(\\"10\\", 5)); // \\"10\\" * 5 = 50 (implicit conversion, which may not be obvious)\\nconsole.log(calculateTotal(\\"ten\\", 5)); // NaN (a runtime error)\\n\\n// TypeScript (static type checking)\\nfunction calculateTotal(price: number, quantity: number): number {\\n    return price * quantity;\\n}\\n// The TypeScript compiler will report an error immediately and refuse to compile the code:\\n// calculateTotal(\\"10\\", 5); // An argument of type \'string\' is not assignable to a parameter of type \'number\'.\\n// The correct call:\\nconsole.log(calculateTotal(10, 5)); // 50"],[1926,"Constants are worth moving into a separate module when they are used in several places in the application and their values are logically related. This improves the readability of the code, because all the key values are gathered in one place. Changing a value requires an edit in only one module, which reduces the risk of errors. The approach also makes testing and refactoring easier.","Moving constants into a separate module is a code-organization practice aimed at centralizing the immutable values used in different parts of an application. It is not merely a mechanical relocation of numbers and strings but a way of making code clearer, more manageable and less error-prone.\\n\\nWhen does it make sense?\\n\\n- Repeated use: if the same string, number or object is used in several modules, files or components.\\n- Logical grouping: when the constants belong to a single subject area — for example, API settings (URL, keys, timeouts), status codes, error messages or UI configuration (colours, sizes).\\n- Simpler changes: if a value may change in the future (for example, the base API URL when the environment changes), it is easier to update in one place.\\n- Better readability: named constants (`MAX_RETRY_ATTEMPTS`, for example) are clearer than \\"magic numbers\\" (3, for example) scattered through the code.\\n\\nA practical example\\nConsider an example in JavaScript/TypeScript. Instead of hard-coding values in different files, we create a `constants.js` or `config.ts` module.\\n\\nNow in any component or module of the application we import these constants:\\n\\nWhere it is applied\\nThis approach is universal and is used in practically every programming paradigm and technology stack: frontend (React, Vue, Angular), backend (Node.js, Python, Java), and also in build configuration (Webpack) and environment settings.\\n\\nConclusion: move constants into a separate module when the values are used in several places, are logically grouped or may change. This is a key practice for creating clean, maintainable and scalable code, especially on medium and large projects.","// constants/api.js\\nexport const API_BASE_URL = \'https://api.example.com/v1\';\\nexport const API_TIMEOUT = 30000; // 30 seconds\\nexport const DEFAULT_PAGE_SIZE = 20;\\n\\n// constants/errors.js\\nexport const ERROR_CODES = {\\n  NOT_FOUND: 404,\\n  UNAUTHORIZED: 401,\\n  SERVER_ERROR: 500\\n};\\n\\n// constants/ui.js\\nexport const COLORS = {\\n  PRIMARY: \'#007bff\',\\n  SUCCESS: \'#28a745\',\\n  DANGER: \'#dc3545\'\\n};\\n\\n// userService.js\\nimport { API_BASE_URL, DEFAULT_PAGE_SIZE } from \'../constants/api\';\\n\\nasync function fetchUsers(page = 1) {\\n  const response = await fetch(\\n    `${API_BASE_URL}/users?page=${page}&limit=${DEFAULT_PAGE_SIZE}`\\n  );\\n  return response.json();\\n}"],[1927,"Stating a function\'s return type explicitly when working with an ORM helps the TypeScript compiler check that the data you expect to get from the database is correct. It prevents runtime errors where, for example, a function is supposed to return an array of objects but returns a single object or null. It also improves autocompletion in the IDE and makes the code clearer for other developers by showing explicitly what the method returns.","When using an ORM (Object-Relational Mapping) in TypeScript, stating a function\'s return type explicitly is not a mere formality but an important practice that directly affects the reliability and maintainability of the code. ORM libraries such as TypeORM, Prisma or Sequelize often return complex promises with generics, and without an explicit annotation TypeScript may infer the type as `any` or as something too general, which deprives you of the benefits of static typing.\\n\\nThe advantages of stating the type explicitly\\n\\n- Type safety: the compiler checks that you handle the query result correctly (for example, that you access fields that exist on the entity).\\n- Error prevention: you see at once if you expect an array of users (`User[]`) while the method returns a single user (`User | null`).\\n- Better DX (Developer Experience): the IDE provides accurate autocompletion for the fields of the returned object.\\n- Documenting the code: the type serves as documentation that helps other developers understand what the function does.\\n\\nA practical example\\nConsider an example with TypeORM and a `User` entity. Without an explicit type TypeScript cannot check operations on the result correctly.\\n\\nWhere this matters most\\nExplicit types are critical for an ORM\'s repository methods (`find`, `findOne`, `save`), for custom queries with `QueryBuilder` and for defining relations between entities. In complex scenarios — for example when using `select` with partial fields, or joins — the return type may not be an entity but a special DTO or interface, which also has to be stated explicitly.\\n\\nConclusion: stating the return type explicitly when working with an ORM is a mandatory practice for creating reliable, maintainable and scalable backend applications in TypeScript. It minimizes errors caused by data mismatches and speeds development up considerably through good tooling support.","import { User } from \'./entity/User\';\\nimport { AppDataSource } from \'./data-source\';\\n\\n// Without an explicit type: the type of result is inferred as any or Promise<any>\\nasync function getUsersBad() {\\n  return await AppDataSource.getRepository(User).find();\\n}\\n\\n// With an explicit type: it is clear that an array of User is returned\\nasync function getUsersGood(): Promise<User[]> {\\n  return await AppDataSource.getRepository(User).find();\\n}\\n\\n// Using the typed function\\nasync function main() {\\n  const users = await getUsersGood();\\n  // The IDE will suggest the fields: users[0].id, users[0].name\\n  users.forEach(user => console.log(user.name)); // Safe access\\n}"],[1928,"Without type annotations code becomes less clear, because it is not obvious which data types a function expects and what it returns. This increases the risk of runtime errors — passing a string instead of a number, for example. Refactoring also becomes harder, as does the work of static-analysis tools such as mypy or the IDE, which cannot warn you about a type mismatch. In the end the code becomes harder to maintain and scale.","Type hints in functions are a way of stating explicitly the expected types of the arguments and of the return value. Their absence creates several key problems in software development.\\n\\nReduced readability and understanding of the code\\nWhen you see a function without annotations, you cannot quickly understand what it does or which data it needs. You have to read the body of the function, the documentation (if there is any) or even run the code to work out the function\'s contract. This slows development down, especially in large teams or when working with someone else\'s code.\\n\\nMore errors at runtime\\nMany errors caused by passing arguments of the wrong type are only discovered while the program is running. For example, a function that expects a number may receive a string and fail with a `TypeError` or — worse — silently produce an incorrect result.\\n\\nDifficulties with refactoring and with tooling\\nModern IDEs and static analysers (mypy for Python, the TypeScript Compiler) rely heavily on type information. Without annotations they cannot:\\n- Offer autocompletion (intellisense) for arguments and return values.\\n- Detect potential errors before the code runs.\\n- Safely perform automatic refactoring — renaming a function parameter, for example.\\n\\nA practical example with annotations\\nHere is how the same function looks with type annotations:\\n\\nWhere this is applied\\nType annotations are critically important in large projects, microservice architectures and library development, where a clear API contract prevents a great many integration errors. They are also the basis for generating documentation (with Sphinx, for example) and for writing more reliable tests.\\n\\nConclusion: using type annotations is especially useful in team development, when maintaining legacy code and in projects where reliability and predictability matter. They reduce the developer\'s cognitive load and let errors be found while the code is being written rather than in production.","def calculate_discount(price, discount_rate):\\n    # Without annotations it is unclear: is price an int/float? Is discount_rate a float (0.1) or a string (\\"10%\\")?\\n    return price * (1 - discount_rate)\\n\\n# The error may only show up at the call site\\nresult = calculate_discount(100, \\"0.1\\")  # TypeError: can\'t multiply sequence by non-int of type \'str\'\\n\\nfrom typing import Union\\n\\ndef calculate_discount(price: Union[int, float], discount_rate: float) -> float:\\n    \\"\\"\\"Returns the price after the discount has been applied.\\"\\"\\"\\n    return price * (1 - discount_rate)\\n\\n# Now the IDE and mypy will warn about the error\\nresult = calculate_discount(100, \\"0.1\\")  # mypy: Argument 2 to \\"calculate_discount\\" has incompatible type \\"str\\"; expected \\"float\\""],[1929,"Union types in TypeScript let a variable, a function parameter or a return value belong to one of several types. This is useful when a value can be, for example, a string or a number, or when a function can return an object of a particular shape or null. Using a union increases the flexibility of the code while keeping compile-time type checking. TypeScript will require every possible variant of the type to be handled, which prevents runtime errors.","Union types are a powerful construct of TypeScript\'s type system that lets a variable, a function parameter or a return value belong not to one type but to several. This reflects real scenarios where a value can take different forms depending on the context.\\n\\nThe core idea and the syntax\\nThe syntax of a union type uses the vertical-bar operator `|` between the types. For example, `string | number` means that the value may be either a string or a number. This lets you describe the domain of possible values precisely without resorting to the `any` type, which switches off type checking.\\n\\nPractical use\\nUnion types are often applied in the following situations:\\n- Functions that work with different types of input: a formatting function, for example, that accepts both strings and numbers.\\n- Handling values that may be absent: instead of returning only an object, a function may return `null` or `undefined` on error — the type of the result would be `SomeType | null`.\\n- Modelling states: a data-loading state, for example, can be represented as a union: `Loading | Success | Error`.\\n- Working with the DOM API: the `document.getElementById` property returns `HTMLElement | null`, because the element may not exist.\\n\\nA code example\\nConsider a function that formats a value for display. It has to work with both numbers and strings:\\n\\nWithout a union type we would have to use `any`, which would deprive us of type checking and autocompletion.\\n\\nType narrowing\\nThe key point when working with a union is narrowing the type. TypeScript will not let you perform an operation specific to one type until you have established which variant you are working with. The `typeof` and `instanceof` checks, equality checks or custom type guards are used for this.\\n\\nUnion types are a fundamental tool for creating flexible yet type-safe interfaces. They let you model a program\'s behaviour precisely without sacrificing strict typing.\\n\\nConclusion: use union types when a value, a parameter or a function\'s result can logically belong to one of several types. This is especially useful for handling optional values, modelling different states and working with external APIs where the data may come in different formats.","function formatValue(value: string | number): string {\\n    // TypeScript forces us to check the type before any operations\\n    if (typeof value === \'string\') {\\n        return value.toUpperCase();\\n    } else {\\n        // Here TypeScript knows that value is a number\\n        return value.toFixed(2);\\n    }\\n}\\n\\nconsole.log(formatValue(\'hello\')); // \\"HELLO\\"\\nconsole.log(formatValue(42.123));  // \\"42.12\\""],[1930,"Type annotations in Python are written after a colon for arguments and after an arrow `->` for the return value. They do not affect how the code runs, but they help developers and tools like mypy understand the expected data types. For example, `def greet(name: str) -> str:`. For complex types use the `typing` module: `List[int]`, `Optional[str]`. Annotations make code self-documenting and easier to maintain.","Type hints in Python are a way of stating explicitly the expected data types for a function\'s arguments and its return value. They were introduced in PEP 484 and serve primarily for static code analysis, better readability and documenting interfaces.\\n\\nThe basic syntax\\nFor an argument the type is given after a colon; for the return value it comes after an arrow `->`, before the function\'s colon.\\n\\nUsing the typing module\\nFor more complex structures such as lists, dictionaries, optional values or user-defined classes, the `typing` module is used.\\n\\nPractical use and tooling\\nType annotations are especially useful in large projects and in team development. They let:\\n- static-analysis tools (mypy, pyright, Pylance) check that types match and find potential errors before the code runs.\\n- IDEs (VS Code, PyCharm) provide more accurate autocompletion and hints.\\n- you write self-documenting code, where a function\'s contract (what it accepts and what it returns) is clear from its signature.\\n\\nIt is important to remember that Python remains a dynamically typed language — annotations do not affect execution at runtime (unless you use an additional library such as pydantic for validation).\\n\\nAn example with a user-defined type\\n\\nIn Python 3.10+ a more concise syntax with a vertical bar can be used instead of `Union[User, int]`: `User | int`.\\n\\nConclusion: type annotations are worth applying to every non-trivial function in projects where readability, maintainability and early error detection matter. They are practically mandatory in enterprise development and when creating libraries with clear APIs.","def add(a: int, b: int) -> int:\\n    return a + b\\n\\n# Use with a type hint for a string\\nmessage: str = \\"Hello\\"\\nprint(add(5, 3))  # 8\\n\\nfrom typing import List, Optional, Dict\\n\\ndef process_items(items: List[int], config: Optional[Dict[str, str]] = None) -> bool:\\n    \\"\\"\\"Processes a list of integers and an optional configuration dictionary.\\"\\"\\"\\n    if not items:\\n        return False\\n    # ... processing logic\\n    return True\\n\\n# Calling the function\\nresult = process_items([1, 2, 3], {\\"mode\\": \\"fast\\"})\\n\\nfrom typing import Union\\n\\nclass User:\\n    def __init__(self, name: str):\\n        self.name = name\\n\\ndef get_identifier(entity: Union[User, int]) -> str:\\n    \\"\\"\\"Accepts either a User object or an integer.\\"\\"\\"\\n    if isinstance(entity, User):\\n        return entity.name\\n    else:\\n        return f\\"ID-{entity}\\"\\n\\n# Use\\nuser = User(\\"Alice\\")\\nprint(get_identifier(user))   # Alice\\nprint(get_identifier(42))     # ID-42"],[1931,"TypeVar is used to create generic types in Python. It lets you declare functions or classes that work with different types while preserving the relationship between them — for example, a function that returns an element of the same type as the argument it was given. This increases type safety and improves the hints in the IDE without restricting the flexibility of the code.","TypeVar is a tool from the typing module for creating type variables, which let you define generic functions, methods and classes. It is useful when the logic of the code has to work with various types while type information still has to be preserved for static checking.\\n\\nThe main uses\\nTypeVar is often used in the following scenarios:\\n- Creating functions where the type of the argument and of the return value are related (for example, an identity function that returns the object it was given).\\n- Defining generic classes such as containers (Box[T], for example) that can hold data of any type while still being type-checked.\\n- Ensuring type safety in algorithms that abstract away from concrete types (comparison or sorting functions, for example).\\n\\nCode examples\\nConsider a simple example of an identity function that returns the argument it was given:\\n\\nA more complex example is a generic Box class that holds a value of an arbitrary type:\\n\\nTypeVar also supports bounds and variance (covariant/contravariant), which lets you create stricter types. For example, constraining a type to numbers only:\\n\\nConclusion: TypeVar is worth applying when developing libraries, frameworks or any code that needs the flexibility to work with different types without losing the benefits of static typing. It is especially useful for creating reusable components such as containers, data-processing utilities or decorators.","from typing import TypeVar, List\\n\\nT = TypeVar(\'T\')  # Declaring a type variable\\n\\ndef identity(value: T) -> T:\\n    return value\\n\\n# Use\\nresult_int: int = identity(42)        # The type T is inferred as int\\nresult_str: str = identity(\\"hello\\")   # The type T is inferred as str\\n\\nfrom typing import TypeVar, Generic\\n\\nT = TypeVar(\'T\')\\n\\nclass Box(Generic[T]):\\n    def __init__(self, content: T) -> None:\\n        self.content = content\\n    \\n    def get_content(self) -> T:\\n        return self.content\\n\\n# Use\\nbox_int: Box[int] = Box(100)\\nbox_str: Box[str] = Box(\\"text\\")\\nprint(box_int.get_content() + 5)   # Works, because content is an int\\n\\nfrom typing import TypeVar, Union\\n\\nNumeric = TypeVar(\'Numeric\', int, float)  # T can only be int or float\\n\\ndef double(x: Numeric) -> Numeric:\\n    return x * 2\\n\\nprint(double(10))     # OK\\nprint(double(3.14))   # OK\\n# double(\\"string\\")    # Type error: a string is not part of Numeric"],[1932,"Generics in Python let you create functions, classes or data structures that can work with different types while preserving type information for checks and hints. They are implemented through the `typing` module using `TypeVar` and generic classes such as `List[T]` or `Dict[K, V]`. This helps static analysers such as mypy check that types are used correctly without losing the flexibility of the code. For example, you can write a function that returns a list of elements of any type, as long as they are all the same.","Generics in Python are a mechanism that lets you define functions, classes or data structures capable of working with various types while preserving information about the concrete type for static checks. They are implemented through the `typing` module, which introduces type annotations that support parametric polymorphism.\\n\\nThe main components of generics\\nThe key elements for creating generics:\\n- `TypeVar`: defines a type variable that can be replaced by a concrete type when used.\\n- Generic classes: `List[T]` or `Dict[K, V]` from the `typing` module, for example, or user-defined classes inheriting from `Generic[T]`.\\n- Annotations: stating generic types in the signatures of functions and classes.\\n\\nExamples of use\\nConsider a simple example of a function that returns the first element of a list of any type:\\n\\nHere `T` is a type variable that ties the type of the list\'s elements to the type of the return value. When called with a `List[int]` the function returns an `int`.\\n\\nCreating a generic class\\nYou can create your own classes parameterized by types. For example, a simple container:\\n\\nThe `Box` class can hold an object of any type, but when creating an instance we state the concrete type, which lets the static analyser check that operations are correct.\\n\\nWhere generics are applied\\nGenerics are especially useful in the following scenarios:\\n- Libraries and frameworks: in FastAPI, for example, for typing requests and responses, or in an ORM for typed database queries.\\n- Data structures: implementing stacks, queues and trees that have to work with elements of different types.\\n- Higher-order functions: functions that accept other functions (callbacks) with typed arguments and return values.\\n- Data-processing utilities: functions for mapping, filtering and reducing collections.\\n\\nConstraints and advanced capabilities\\nWith `TypeVar` you can set bounds or require the type to be of a particular class. For example:\\n\\nYou can also use `typing.Generic` with several parameters — for a dictionary, for example: `Dict[K, V]`.\\n\\nConclusion: generics in Python through the `typing` module are worth applying when you need to create flexible, reusable code that must remain type-safe. They are especially useful in libraries, frameworks and any project that uses static type checking (mypy, pyright), since they help avoid type errors and improve the readability of the code.","from typing import TypeVar, List\\n\\nT = TypeVar(\'T\')  # Defining a type variable\\n\\ndef first_element(items: List[T]) -> T:\\n    \\"\\"\\"Returns the first element of the list.\\"\\"\\"\\n    return items[0]\\n\\n# Use\\nnumbers: List[int] = [1, 2, 3]\\nresult: int = first_element(numbers)  # mypy understands that result has the type int\\nprint(result)  # 1\\n\\nfrom typing import Generic, TypeVar\\n\\nT = TypeVar(\'T\')\\n\\nclass Box(Generic[T]):\\n    def __init__(self, content: T) -> None:\\n        self.content = content\\n    \\n    def get_content(self) -> T:\\n        return self.content\\n\\n# Use\\nbox_int: Box[int] = Box(42)\\nvalue: int = box_int.get_content()  # The type of value is inferred as int\\n\\nbox_str: Box[str] = Box(\\"hello\\")\\ntext: str = box_str.get_content()   # The type of text is inferred as str\\n\\nfrom typing import TypeVar, List\\n\\n# T must be a subtype of float or int (a numeric type)\\nNumeric = TypeVar(\'Numeric\', int, float)\\n\\ndef sum_values(values: List[Numeric]) -> Numeric:\\n    return sum(values)\\n\\n# Correct\\nsum_values([1, 2, 3])   # int\\nsum_values([1.5, 2.5])  # float\\n\\n# A type error (if checked with mypy): str is not part of int/float\\n# sum_values([\\"a\\", \\"b\\"])"],[1933,"Using exceptions for business logic has both pros and cons. The pros: they separate the normal flow from errors, making code cleaner, and they let you handle errors centrally at the upper levels. The cons: exceptions add a performance overhead and can hide logic, making it less explicit. For expected errors (invalid input, for example) it is better to use return values or special result objects.","Exceptions are an error-handling mechanism that interrupts the normal flow of a program and transfers control to a special handler (a catch block). In the context of business logic, which implements the rules and operations of a domain, it is important to distinguish clearly when exceptions are appropriate and when they are not.\\n\\nThe advantages of using exceptions\\n\\n- Separation of concerns: the business-logic code stays clean, because error handling is moved to a higher level (to the controller or the presentation layer, for example).\\n- Centralized handling: you can catch exceptions in one place, log them and turn them into responses for the user (HTTP 400, for example).\\n- Enforced handling: in languages with checked exceptions (such as Java) the compiler forces the developer to handle the possible errors explicitly, which increases reliability.\\n\\nThe disadvantages of using exceptions\\n\\n- Performance: creating an exception and \\"unwinding\\" the stack is usually more expensive than returning a simple value. This can be critical in high-load loops.\\n- Obscured control flow: exceptions can be used to control the normal flow (for example, to break out of nested loops), which is considered an anti-pattern because it hides intent.\\n- Implicitness: it is not always obvious to the calling code which exceptions may be thrown, especially in languages without checked exceptions (such as JavaScript or Python).\\n\\nA practical example\\nConsider a function that checks a user\'s balance before a withdrawal. Using an exception for an expected situation (insufficient funds) may be excessive.\\n\\nAn alternative approach is returning a result object that explicitly indicates success or failure, which can be more efficient and clearer for expected failures.\\n\\nConclusion: exceptions are worth using for handling genuinely exceptional, unexpected situations (a database failure, a lost network connection). For expected business-logic errors (invalid input, insufficient funds) it is preferable to use return values or special result types (Result/Option), since this makes the control flow explicit and reduces overhead.","// An example in TypeScript: using an exception for a business rule\\nclass InsufficientFundsError extends Error {\\n    constructor(public userId: number, public required: number) {\\n        super(`User ${userId} lacks ${required} credits.`);\\n    }\\n}\\n\\nfunction withdraw(userId: number, amount: number): void {\\n    const balance = getBalance(userId); // get the balance from the database\\n    if (balance < amount) {\\n        // A business error: insufficient funds\\n        throw new InsufficientFundsError(userId, amount);\\n    }\\n    // ... withdrawal logic\\n}\\n\\n// Handling at the top level (in an Express route, for example)\\ntry {\\n    withdraw(123, 1000);\\n    res.status(200).json({ success: true });\\n} catch (error) {\\n    if (error instanceof InsufficientFundsError) {\\n        res.status(400).json({ error: error.message });\\n    } else {\\n        // An unexpected error (a database problem, for example)\\n        res.status(500).json({ error: \'Internal server error\' });\\n    }\\n}\\n\\n// The alternative: returning a result without an exception\\ninterface WithdrawalResult {\\n    success: boolean;\\n    errorMessage?: string;\\n}\\n\\nfunction withdrawSafe(userId: number, amount: number): WithdrawalResult {\\n    const balance = getBalance(userId);\\n    if (balance < amount) {\\n        return { success: false, errorMessage: \'Insufficient funds\' };\\n    }\\n    // ... withdrawal logic\\n    return { success: true };\\n}\\n\\nconst result = withdrawSafe(123, 1000);\\nif (!result.success) {\\n    // Handle the expected business error\\n    console.log(result.errorMessage);\\n}"],[1934,"For code quality control people often use ESLint for static analysis and Husky to automate checks through Git hooks. ESLint checks code against the configured style rules and for potential errors. Husky lets you run those checks (`npm run lint`, for example) automatically before a commit, preventing problematic code from reaching the repository. This forms a protective barrier and keeps the team disciplined.","Code quality control is a set of practices aimed at keeping a codebase clean, consistent and reliable. The two key technologies for this in the JavaScript/TypeScript ecosystem are the ESLint linter and Husky, a tool for managing Git hooks.\\n\\nESLint: static code analysis\\nESLint analyses your JavaScript or TypeScript code without executing it, looking for errors, style problems and discouraged constructs. It works from a set of rules that can be configured. Popular configurations such as `eslint:recommended` or the Airbnb style provide ready-made rule sets.\\n\\nESLint is applied during development: it can be run by hand, integrated into a code editor (VS Code, for example) to highlight problems in real time, or run as part of a CI/CD pipeline.\\n\\nHusky: automation through Git hooks\\nHusky simplifies working with Git hooks — scripts that Git runs at particular moments (before a commit or before a push, for example). Husky lets you configure those hooks easily right inside the project, making the process standardized and mandatory for every developer.\\n\\nThe typical use is a pre-commit hook that runs linting and possibly formatting (Prettier) or unit tests. If the script exits with an error, the commit is aborted.\\n\\nTogether these tools create an automated quality-control pipeline. A developer trying to make a commit automatically triggers a code check. This prevents code that violates the team\'s conventions or contains obvious syntax errors from reaching the shared branch.\\n\\nConclusion: the practice of using ESLint with Husky is especially useful in team development for keeping code consistent and implementing the \\"don\'t break master\\" principle. It is worth applying on any project where more than one person works on the code, or which is meant to be maintained for a long time.","// An example .eslintrc.js configuration\\nmodule.exports = {\\n  env: {\\n    browser: true,\\n    es2021: true,\\n  },\\n  extends: [\'eslint:recommended\', \'plugin:@typescript-eslint/recommended\'],\\n  parser: \'@typescript-eslint/parser\',\\n  plugins: [\'@typescript-eslint\'],\\n  rules: {\\n    \'no-console\': \'warn\', // A warning when console.log is used\\n    \'semi\': [\'error\', \'always\'], // Requires a semicolon\\n  },\\n};\\n\\n// An example of the setup in package.json after installing Husky\\n{\\n  \\"scripts\\": {\\n    \\"lint\\": \\"eslint src/**/*.{js,ts}\\",\\n    \\"prepare\\": \\"husky install\\"\\n  },\\n  \\"devDependencies\\": {\\n    \\"eslint\\": \\"^8.0.0\\",\\n    \\"husky\\": \\"^8.0.0\\"\\n  }\\n}\\n// Then the hook is created in the terminal:\\n// npx husky add .husky/pre-commit \\"npm run lint\\""],[1935,"Generics let you create components that work with various types rather than with one concrete type. You declare a type parameter (`<T>`, for example) in the definition of a type, interface or function. When using that type you can state the concrete type that will replace the parameter. This makes the code type-safe and reusable, avoiding duplicated logic for different data types.","Generics are a powerful TypeScript tool for creating components that can work with various data types while preserving type information. Instead of hard-coding a type (number or string, for example), you use a type parameter that will be replaced by a concrete type when the component is used.\\n\\nThe basic syntax and creating custom types\\nA type parameter is usually denoted by a single letter, `T` (for Type), for example. It can be used when defining a type, an interface or a class.\\n\\nGenerics in functions and interfaces\\nGenerics are especially useful for functions that have to return a value of the same type as the argument, or for describing data structures such as arrays or promises.\\n\\nGeneric constraints\\nSometimes you need to guarantee that a type parameter has certain properties. The `extends` keyword is used for this.\\n\\nPractical use\\nGenerics are widely used in:\\n- Libraries and frameworks: React (for typing props and state, for example), Redux Toolkit.\\n- Data structures: creating typed queues, stacks and linked lists.\\n- Working with APIs: typing server responses, where the structure of the data is known but can differ between endpoints.\\n\\nConclusion: use generics to create flexible, reusable and type-safe custom types, especially when a component\'s logic is the same for different data types but you want to preserve information about the concrete type at compile time.","// A generic type\\ntype Container<T> = {\\n  value: T;\\n  id: number;\\n};\\n\\n// Use with concrete types\\nconst stringContainer: Container<string> = { value: \\"Hello\\", id: 1 };\\nconst numberContainer: Container<number> = { value: 42, id: 2 };\\n\\n// A generic function\\nfunction identity<T>(arg: T): T {\\n  return arg;\\n}\\nconst output = identity<string>(\\"myString\\"); // The type of output is string\\n\\n// A generic interface for an API response\\ninterface ApiResponse<T> {\\n  data: T;\\n  status: number;\\n  message: string;\\n}\\n\\nconst userResponse: ApiResponse<{ name: string; age: number }> = {\\n  data: { name: \\"Alice\\", age: 30 },\\n  status: 200,\\n  message: \\"OK\\"\\n};\\n\\ninterface HasLength {\\n  length: number;\\n}\\n\\nfunction logLength<T extends HasLength>(arg: T): void {\\n  console.log(arg.length); // Safe, because T is guaranteed to have length\\n}\\n\\nlogLength(\\"text\\"); // OK, a string has a length property\\nlogLength([1, 2, 3]); // OK, an array has length\\n// logLength(42); // Error: a number has no length property"],[1936,"Optional is a container that may hold a value or be empty. With generics we parameterize the type of the value that can sit inside the Optional. This lets you create type-safe wrappers for any data type. For example, an Optional may contain a string or be empty. The implementation includes a private field for storing the value and methods for checking whether it is present and for extracting it.","The Optional (or Maybe) concept is a design pattern meant to solve the problem of working with missing values (null) in a safer, more expressive way. Instead of returning null and risking a NullPointerException, a method returns a container that explicitly reports whether a value is present. Generics let you make this container universal, so that it can work with any data type while still being type-checked at compile time.\\n\\nThe main components of the implementation\\nA basic implementation of Optional using generics usually includes the following elements:\\n- A private field for storing a value of the generic type T.\\n- A private constructor that takes a value (or null).\\n- Static factory methods for creating instances: `of(value)` for non-null values and `empty()` for an empty Optional.\\n- Methods for checking whether a value is present: `isPresent()`.\\n- Methods for extracting the value safely: `get()` (throws an exception if there is no value), `orElse(defaultValue)`, `orElseGet(supplier)`.\\n- Methods for functional transformations: `map()`, `flatMap()`, `filter()`.\\n\\nA code example in Java\\nHere is a simplified implementation of Optional using generics:\\n\\nThis implementation shows how generics (`<T>`) let the Optional class work with any type. For example, you can create `Optional<String>`, `Optional<Integer>` and so on, and the compiler will check that the types match.\\n\\nWhere it is applied\\nOptional is widely used in modern languages and frameworks to indicate explicitly that a value may be absent. In Java 8+ it is built into the standard library (`java.util.Optional`). In TypeScript/JavaScript a similar pattern is implemented with union types or libraries (fp-ts, for example). In functional languages (Haskell, Scala) it is a fundamental part of the type system.\\n\\nUsing Optional makes code more readable and safer, because it forces the developer to handle the case of a missing value explicitly instead of silently swallowing null.\\n\\nConclusion: implementing Optional with generics is a powerful way of creating a type-safe container for optional values. It is worth applying in the APIs of methods whose return value may be absent, in order to avoid null-related errors and make the method\'s contract more explicit.","public class Optional<T> {\\n    private final T value; // The stored value of type T\\n\\n    // A private constructor\\n    private Optional(T value) {\\n        this.value = value;\\n    }\\n\\n    // Create an Optional with a non-null value\\n    public static <T> Optional<T> of(T value) {\\n        if (value == null) {\\n            throw new NullPointerException(\\"Value cannot be null\\");\\n        }\\n        return new Optional<>(value);\\n    }\\n\\n    // Create an empty Optional\\n    public static <T> Optional<T> empty() {\\n        return new Optional<>(null);\\n    }\\n\\n    // Check whether there is a value\\n    public boolean isPresent() {\\n        return value != null;\\n    }\\n\\n    // Get the value (dangerous if empty)\\n    public T get() {\\n        if (value == null) {\\n            throw new NoSuchElementException(\\"No value present\\");\\n        }\\n        return value;\\n    }\\n\\n    // Return the value or a default\\n    public T orElse(T other) {\\n        return value != null ? value : other;\\n    }\\n}"],[1937,"A hard dependency on a concrete implementation such as a Singleton can be removed by applying the dependency inversion principle (DIP) and dependency injection (DI). Instead of the class creating the dependency\'s instance itself, it should receive it from outside through a constructor, a method or a property. This lets you substitute the real implementation with a mock for testing, or with a different implementation depending on the context. The code thus becomes less coupled and more flexible.","A hard dependency on a concrete class, especially on a Singleton, creates problems with testing and with the system\'s flexibility, because the code becomes tightly coupled to one implementation. The solution is to apply the dependency inversion principle (DIP), which states that high-level modules should not depend on low-level modules; both should depend on abstractions.\\n\\nThe main approach: Dependency Injection\\nInstead of a class creating the dependency inside itself, it should receive it from outside. This can be done through the constructor (the most preferable way), through a setter or through an interface. Consider an example in TypeScript.\\n\\nWhere and how this is applied\\nThis approach is widely used in modern frameworks (Angular, NestJS, Spring). Dependency-injection containers (IoC containers) manage the creation and wiring of objects automatically. In the example above `OrderService` now depends on the abstraction `ILogger` rather than on the concrete `Logger`. This lets you:\\n- Test the class easily by substituting the dependencies with mock objects.\\n- Change the implementation (for example, replace a console logger with a file logger or Sentry) without changing the code of `OrderService`.\\n- Respect the single-responsibility principle (SRP) — the class is not in the business of creating its own dependencies.\\n\\nConclusion: apply dependency injection through abstractions (interfaces or abstract classes) everywhere the implementation may change or isolated testing is required. This is especially important for services that work with external systems (databases, APIs, logging).","// BAD: a hard dependency on a Singleton\\nclass OrderService {\\n    private logger = Logger.getInstance(); // Using the Singleton directly\\n\\n    processOrder() {\\n        this.logger.log(\'Processing order...\');\\n        // ... logic\\n    }\\n}\\n\\n// GOOD: the dependency is injected through the constructor\\ninterface ILogger {\\n    log(message: string): void;\\n}\\n\\nclass ConsoleLogger implements ILogger {\\n    log(message: string) {\\n        console.log(`[LOG]: ${message}`);\\n    }\\n}\\n\\nclass OrderService {\\n    constructor(private logger: ILogger) {} // The dependency is supplied from outside\\n\\n    processOrder() {\\n        this.logger.log(\'Processing order...\');\\n        // ... logic\\n    }\\n}\\n\\n// Use\\nconst logger = new ConsoleLogger();\\nconst orderService = new OrderService(logger);\\norderService.processOrder();\\n\\n// For testing the implementation is easy to substitute\\nclass MockLogger implements ILogger {\\n    log(message: string) {\\n        // Do nothing, or record into an array for assertions\\n    }\\n}\\nconst testService = new OrderService(new MockLogger());"],[1938,"Managing the dependencies of the network layer means designing it so that it is not tightly coupled to a concrete implementation. The main approach is to use abstractions (interfaces) for network clients together with dependency injection. This lets you substitute real HTTP requests with mocks for tests, change libraries (axios for fetch, for example) without rewriting the business logic, and manage configuration centrally (base URLs, headers). Such a layer becomes an independent module.","Managing the dependencies of the network layer correctly is a key aspect of a modern application\'s architecture. It separates the code that performs HTTP requests from the rest of the business logic, making the system more modular, more testable and more adaptable to change.\\n\\nThe main principles\\n\\n- Abstraction: define an interface (or an abstract class) for the network client. The business logic should depend on that interface rather than on the concrete axios or fetch class.\\n- Dependency injection (DI): the concrete implementation of the network client is \\"injected\\" from outside, usually through a constructor or method parameters.\\n- Single responsibility: the network layer should deal only with communication: forming requests, handling responses and errors. Parsing data and business rules are the responsibility of other modules.\\n\\nA practical implementation\\nConsider an example in TypeScript. First we define the abstraction, then the implementation, and then we show how to use it.\\n\\nThe advantages and the uses\\nThis approach is applied for:\\n- Unit testing: you can pass a mock `HttpClient` into `UserService` that returns fake data without making real network requests.\\n- Easy replacement of libraries: to move from fetch to axios you need to create a new `AxiosHttpClient` class implementing the same interface and change one line where the dependencies are created.\\n- Centralized management: the configuration (baseURL, authentication headers, interceptors) is encapsulated in one place — in the `HttpClient` implementation.\\n\\nConclusion: managing dependencies through abstractions and DI is worth applying in any project where network requests are a critical part. It is mandatory for medium and large applications, since it considerably simplifies testing, maintenance and the evolution of the code when external APIs or network libraries change.","// 1. The abstraction (interface) of the network client\\ninterface HttpClient {\\n  get<T>(url: string): Promise<T>;\\n  post<T>(url: string, data: unknown): Promise<T>;\\n  // ... other methods\\n}\\n\\n// 2. A concrete implementation using fetch (or axios)\\nclass FetchHttpClient implements HttpClient {\\n  private baseURL: string;\\n\\n  constructor(baseURL: string) {\\n    this.baseURL = baseURL;\\n  }\\n\\n  async get<T>(url: string): Promise<T> {\\n    const response = await fetch(`${this.baseURL}${url}`);\\n    if (!response.ok) {\\n      throw new Error(`HTTP error! status: ${response.status}`);\\n    }\\n    return response.json() as Promise<T>;\\n  }\\n  // ... the implementation of post and the other methods\\n}\\n\\n// 3. The business-logic service, which depends on the abstraction\\nclass UserService {\\n  constructor(private httpClient: HttpClient) {}\\n\\n  async getUsers() {\\n    // The business logic uses only the interface\\n    return this.httpClient.get<User[]>(\'/users\');\\n  }\\n}\\n\\n// 4. Composing (\\"wiring up\\") the dependencies\\nconst httpClient = new FetchHttpClient(\'https://api.example.com\');\\nconst userService = new UserService(httpClient);\\n\\n// 5. Use\\n// const users = await userService.getUsers();"],[1939,"When handling JSON with missing fields you need to allow for two main scenarios: when the field is absent entirely and when it has the value null. In strictly typed languages such as TypeScript you can use optional types or default values. In JavaScript you can use the optional chaining operator (?.) or the nullish coalescing operator (??) for safe access. It is always worth validating incoming data to avoid runtime errors.","When working with JSON, especially from external sources, you cannot guarantee that every expected field is present. Handling missing data is a key part of building fault-tolerant applications.\\n\\nThe main approaches to handling it\\n\\n- Checking whether a field exists: before accessing a value you need to make sure the field exists on the object.\\n- Using default values: if a field is absent or equal to null, a safe default should be substituted.\\n- Validating the structure: using libraries to check that the JSON matches the expected schema (JSON Schema, Zod, Joi).\\n\\nExamples in JavaScript/TypeScript\\nConsider a typical scenario of parsing a response from an API.\\n\\nValidation with libraries\\nFor complex structures it is better to use validation libraries. For example, with Zod in TypeScript:\\n\\nConclusion: handling missing fields is a mandatory practice for preventing failures. Use safe-access operators and default values for simple cases, and validation schemas for complex data structures, especially when the JSON comes from unreliable sources.","// The original JSON, where the \'age\' field may be absent\\nconst jsonString = \'{\\"name\\": \\"Alice\\"}\';\\nconst data = JSON.parse(jsonString);\\n\\n// UNSAFE: may throw an error if the field is absent\\n// const userAge = data.age;\\n\\n// Safe access with the ?. and ?? operators\\nconst userAge = data?.age ?? 25; // If age is missing or null, it will be 25\\nconsole.log(`Age: ${userAge}`); // Output: Age: 25\\n\\n// An explicit check\\nlet userName;\\nif (\'name\' in data) {\\n    userName = data.name;\\n} else {\\n    userName = \'Guest\';\\n}\\n\\n// Using destructuring with default values\\nconst { name = \'Guest\', age = 25, city = \'Unknown\' } = data;\\nconsole.log(name, age, city); // Alice 25 Unknown\\n\\nimport { z } from \'zod\';\\n\\n// We define the schema of the expected data\\nconst UserSchema = z.object({\\n    name: z.string().default(\'Guest\'),\\n    age: z.number().optional().default(25),\\n    email: z.string().email().optional(),\\n});\\n\\n// We parse and validate the incoming data\\ntry {\\n    const safeUser = UserSchema.parse(incomingData);\\n    // safeUser is guaranteed to have the name and age fields with their default values\\n    console.log(safeUser.name);\\n} catch (err) {\\n    // Handling the validation error\\n    console.error(\'Invalid data:\', err);\\n}"],[1940,"If you do not use await when calling an asynchronous function, then instead of waiting for the result of the operation the function returns a Promise object in the pending state. This means the code carries on executing synchronously without waiting for the asynchronous task to finish, which can lead to an unresolved value being used. For example, if the fetchData function returns a Promise, then without await the variable receives the Promise itself rather than the data inside it, which will cause errors when you try to work with it as an ordinary object.","Asynchronous functions in JavaScript, declared with the `async` keyword, always return a Promise object. The `await` keyword is used inside such functions to suspend execution until the Promise is fulfilled or rejected. If you call an asynchronous function without `await`, you get that Promise directly rather than its result.\\n\\nWhat happens without await\\nWhen an asynchronous function is called without `await`, it starts executing but control returns to the calling code immediately. This means that subsequent operations can run before the asynchronous task has finished, which leads to a race condition or to data that is not ready being used.\\n\\nA code example\\n\\nWhere this is applied\\nSometimes a deliberate call without `await` is useful — when you need to start several asynchronous operations in parallel to improve performance. For example, you can start several requests at once and then use `Promise.all` to wait for all the results.\\n\\nConclusion\\nUse `await` to wait for the result of an asynchronous function when the code that follows depends on that result. If you need to perform operations in parallel or to manage the Promise by hand, you can omit `await`, but then the Promise must be handled explicitly with the `.then()` or `.catch()` methods.","async function fetchData() {\\n    // Simulating an asynchronous operation, an API request for example\\n    return new Promise(resolve => setTimeout(() => resolve(\'Data received\'), 1000));\\n}\\n\\nasync function process() {\\n    // A call without await\\n    const dataPromise = fetchData();\\n    console.log(dataPromise); // Prints: Promise { <pending> }\\n    // Trying to use the Promise as a string will cause an error\\n    // console.log(dataPromise.toUpperCase()); // Error!\\n    \\n    // The correct call with await\\n    const data = await fetchData();\\n    console.log(data); // Prints: \'Data received\' (after one second)\\n}\\n\\nprocess();"],[1941,"In TypeScript an enum can implement an interface. This lets you guarantee that every value of the enumeration conforms to a particular structure. For example, you can create an interface for an order status and then implement it through an enum, so that every value has code and description fields. This approach improves type safety and documents the code.","In TypeScript enums are not merely a set of numeric or string constants. They can behave like objects at runtime and like types at compile time. One of the advanced capabilities is implementing interfaces with an enum. This lets you impose additional constraints on the structure of the enumeration\'s values, ensuring consistency and type safety.\\n\\nHow it works\\nAn interface in TypeScript defines a contract — the set of properties and methods an object must have. An enum essentially creates an object whose keys are the names of the enumeration\'s members and whose values are the corresponding constants. If the enum\'s values are objects (for example, in the case of computed or string enums with object values), they can be checked against an interface.\\n\\nA code example\\nConsider a practical example where we need to define order statuses with additional information.\\n\\nWhere this is applied\\nImplementing interfaces through an enum is useful in the following scenarios:\\n- Type-safe configurations: when a set of constants has to share a single structure (error codes with messages and HTTP statuses, for example).\\n- Documentation and maintenance: the interface describes explicitly which data is associated with each enum value, which makes the code clearer.\\n- Integration with type systems: it lets you use an enum in functions that expect parameters of a particular interface, strengthening the compiler\'s checks.\\n\\nIt is important to note that the syntax `enum MyEnum implements MyInterface` is not directly supported in TypeScript for plain numeric or string enums, because their values are primitives. However, by using objects as values (an enum with computed object values, for example) or by combining an enum with a namespace, you can achieve the same effect.\\n\\nConclusion: use enums with interfaces when you need a strictly typed set of related constants that must carry additional structured information — for statuses, error codes or configuration objects in an application, for example.","// We define the interface every status must implement\\ninterface StatusInfo {\\n  code: number;\\n  description: string;\\n}\\n\\n// We create an enum that implements this interface\\nenum OrderStatus implements StatusInfo {\\n  Pending = 10,\\n  Processing = 20,\\n  Shipped = 30,\\n  Delivered = 40,\\n  Cancelled = 99\\n\\n  // To implement the interface we would need to add methods or getters.\\n  // But an enum cannot contain methods in its body directly.\\n  // Instead we can use a namespace or a companion object.\\n}\\n\\n// A more practical approach is to use a companion object.\\nnamespace OrderStatus {\\n  export function getInfo(status: OrderStatus): StatusInfo {\\n    switch (status) {\\n      case OrderStatus.Pending:\\n        return { code: 10, description: \\"The order is awaiting processing\\" };\\n      case OrderStatus.Processing:\\n        return { code: 20, description: \\"The order is being assembled\\" };\\n      // ... the remaining cases\\n      default:\\n        return { code: 0, description: \\"Unknown status\\" };\\n    }\\n  }\\n}\\n\\n// Use\\nconst status = OrderStatus.Pending;\\nconst info = OrderStatus.getInfo(status);\\nconsole.log(info.description); // \\"The order is awaiting processing\\""],[1942,"Declaration files (.d.ts) are needed to describe the types of existing JavaScript code, especially of external libraries. They let the TypeScript compiler perform type checking and provide autocompletion in the code editor when those libraries are used. Without them TypeScript would not know the structure of imported JS modules, and development would be less safe and less convenient. These files contain only declarations of types, interfaces and functions, not executable code.","TypeScript declaration files (with the .d.ts extension) serve as a bridge between the statically typed world of TypeScript and the untyped or dynamically typed world of JavaScript. They let you describe the shape of existing JavaScript modules, libraries or global objects without rewriting their source code in TypeScript.\\n\\nThe main purpose and uses\\nThe main task is to provide type safety and improve the development experience (IntelliSense) when using third-party libraries written in JavaScript. For example, when you install a library through npm, TypeScript looks for the corresponding .d.ts file in order to understand which functions, classes and objects it exports and what the types of their parameters and return values are.\\n\\nStructure and creation\\nA .d.ts file contains only type declarations: interfaces, types, declarations of functions, classes and modules. There is no executable code in them. They can be created by hand for your own projects, or you can use ready-made ones from the DefinitelyTyped repository (@types/*).\\n\\nPractical use\\nIn a TypeScript project .d.ts files are usually picked up automatically if they are located in the @types folder or next to the source code. The tsc compiler includes them in the type-checking process. If a library has no official types, the community often publishes them under the @types namespace on npm.\\n\\nConclusion: .d.ts declaration files are critically important for the TypeScript ecosystem, because they let you use the huge legacy of JavaScript libraries safely and with full tooling support, providing type checking and autocompletion without any need to rewrite the code.","// An example declarations.d.ts file for a hypothetical library\\n\\ndeclare module \'my-js-library\' {\\n    export function calculateSum(a: number, b: number): number;\\n    export const DEFAULT_TIMEOUT: number;\\n    export interface Config {\\n        retries: number;\\n        logLevel: \'debug\' | \'info\';\\n    }\\n}"],[1943,"The extends keyword in TypeScript is used for inheritance. In classes it lets one class inherit the properties and methods of another. In interfaces it extends one interface with another, combining their descriptions. In generics extends acts as a constraint, stating that the type parameter must be compatible with the given type. It is the foundation for building hierarchies and reusing code.","The `extends` keyword in TypeScript is a powerful tool for creating \\"is a\\" relationships between types, classes and even the parameters of generic types. It lets you build hierarchies, avoid code duplication and ensure strict typing in complex structures.\\n\\nClass inheritance\\nWhen used with classes, `extends` implements classic OOP inheritance. The child class receives all the properties and methods of the parent (except the private ones) and can add its own or override existing ones with `super`.\\n\\nExtending interfaces\\nFor interfaces `extends` means combining type descriptions. You can extend several interfaces separated by commas, creating a new type that includes all the parents\' properties.\\n\\nGeneric constraints\\nIn generic types `extends` sets a constraint, guaranteeing that the given type will have a particular structure. This lets you access properties safely inside a generic function or class.\\n\\nConclusion: use `extends` to create logical hierarchies of types and classes when you need to express an \\"is a kind of\\" relationship. It is the key mechanism for reusing code, ensuring type safety and modelling a domain in TypeScript.","class Animal {\\n  constructor(public name: string) {}\\n  move(distance: number = 0) {\\n    console.log(`${this.name} moved ${distance}m.`);\\n  }\\n}\\n\\nclass Dog extends Animal {\\n  bark() {\\n    console.log(\'Woof! Woof!\');\\n  }\\n  // Overriding the method\\n  move(distance = 5) {\\n    console.log(\'Running...\');\\n    super.move(distance); // Calling the parent\'s method\\n  }\\n}\\n\\nconst dog = new Dog(\'Rex\');\\ndog.bark(); // Woof! Woof!\\ndog.move(); // Running...\\n            // Rex moved 5m.\\n\\ninterface Named {\\n  name: string;\\n}\\ninterface Aged {\\n  age: number;\\n}\\n// The Person interface includes the properties of both Named and Aged\\ninterface Person extends Named, Aged {\\n  occupation: string;\\n}\\n\\nconst user: Person = {\\n  name: \'Alice\',\\n  age: 30,\\n  occupation: \'Engineer\'\\n}; // Correct\\n\\n// The function accepts only objects that have a length property\\nfunction logLength<T extends { length: number }>(item: T): void {\\n  console.log(item.length);\\n}\\n\\nlogLength(\'hello\'); // 5 (a string has .length)\\nlogLength([1, 2, 3]); // 3 (an array has .length)\\n// logLength(42); // Error: a number has no .length"],[1944,"The result of compiling TypeScript is pure JavaScript code. The TypeScript compiler (tsc) transforms .ts files into .js files, removing every TypeScript syntactic construct such as types and interfaces. This process can also include transpiling the code down to older versions of ECMAScript for browser compatibility. The end result is files that can run in any environment that supports JavaScript.","TypeScript is a superset of JavaScript that adds static typing and other features absent from standard JS. Browsers and the Node.js runtime, however, do not understand TypeScript directly. That is why TypeScript code has to be transformed into standard JavaScript before it runs. This process is called compilation (or often transpilation).\\n\\nWhat happens during compilation?\\nThe compiler\'s main task (tsc) is to remove all type annotations and TypeScript-specific constructs and leave valid JavaScript. For example, interfaces and type aliases disappear from the final code entirely, because they are used only during type checking.\\n\\nA code example\\nConsider a simple TypeScript file (example.ts):\\n\\nAfter compiling with the command `tsc example.ts`, an example.js file will be created. Its contents (for the ES5 target, for example) will look like this:\\n\\nAs you can see, the type annotations and the declaration of the `User` type have been removed. The template-string syntax may have been converted into concatenation for compatibility with older standards.\\n\\nAdditional outputs of compilation\\n\\n- Declaration files (.d.ts): with the `--declaration` option the compiler generates .d.ts files containing only type information. They are useful for other projects that use your compiled code.\\n- Source maps (.js.map): with the `--sourceMap` option .map files are created that link the compiled JS to the original TS for debugging.\\n- Configuration: the process is controlled by the tsconfig.json file, where you can set the target JS version, the strictness of the checks, the output paths and much more.\\n\\nConclusion: the result of compiling TypeScript is executable JavaScript code, ready to run in the browser or in Node.js. This process is necessary in order to enjoy the benefits of static typing during development without losing compatibility with the entire JavaScript ecosystem.","// The original TypeScript code\\ntype User = {\\n  name: string;\\n  age: number;\\n};\\n\\nfunction greet(user: User): string {\\n  return `Hello, ${user.name}!`;\\n}\\n\\nconst john: User = { name: \\"John\\", age: 30 };\\nconsole.log(greet(john));\\n\\n// The compiled JavaScript code\\nfunction greet(user) {\\n    return \\"Hello, \\" + user.name + \\"!\\";\\n}\\nvar john = { name: \\"John\\", age: 30 };\\nconsole.log(greet(john));"],[1945,"In TypeScript an optional generic parameter can be declared by giving it a default value. The syntax resembles default function parameters: `<T = DefaultType>`. This lets you use a generic type, and if it is not stated explicitly the default type is used. The approach makes components more flexible, because they can work both with a concrete type and with the default one when the user does not state it.","Generics in TypeScript let you create components that can work with various data types while preserving type information. Sometimes we want a generic parameter to be optional — that is, if the user does not state it, a sensible default should be used. This is achieved with the syntax for assigning a default value to a generic parameter.\\n\\nThe syntax of an optional generic\\nThe basic syntax looks like this: you declare the generic parameter and immediately assign it a default type with an equals sign.\\n\\nWhere this is applied\\nOptional generics are especially useful in libraries and utilities where you want to provide flexibility but also to have safe default behaviour. For example:\\n- In utility functions such as a `createStore` for state, where the default state may be an empty object.\\n- In UI components (React, Vue), where props can be generic but have sensible defaults.\\n- In decorators or factories that can work with different types but most often with one particular one.\\n\\nAn example with an interface and classes\\nOptional generics can be used not only in functions but also in interfaces, types and classes.\\n\\nCombining with constraints\\nYou can combine optional generics with constraints using the `extends` keyword. The default value must then satisfy the constraint.\\n\\nConclusion: use optional generics with a default type when you are creating universal constructs (functions, types, components) that should work \\"out of the box\\" without an explicit type while still allowing fine-tuning for advanced scenarios. This improves the developer experience by reducing boilerplate in simple cases.","// A function with an optional generic. The default type is string.\\nfunction createArray<T = string>(length: number, value: T): T[] {\\n  return Array(length).fill(value);\\n}\\n\\n// Use with an explicit type (number).\\nconst numArray = createArray<number>(3, 42); // Type: number[]\\n\\n// Use without stating the type — string will be used.\\nconst strArray = createArray(3, \\"hello\\"); // Type: string[]\\n\\n// An API response interface with an optional generic for the data.\\ninterface ApiResponse<TData = Record<string, unknown>> {\\n  success: boolean;\\n  data: TData;\\n}\\n\\n// Use with a concrete type.\\nconst userResponse: ApiResponse<{ name: string }> = {\\n  success: true,\\n  data: { name: \\"Alice\\" }\\n};\\n\\n// Use without a type — data will be Record<string, unknown>.\\nconst basicResponse: ApiResponse = {\\n  success: false,\\n  data: { errorCode: 404 }\\n};\\n\\n// A generic with a constraint (it must be an object) and a default value.\\nfunction mergeObjects<T extends object = {}>(obj1: T, obj2: T): T {\\n  return { ...obj1, ...obj2 };\\n}\\n\\n// Works with any object.\\nconst result1 = mergeObjects({ a: 1 }, { b: 2 }); // Type: { a: number, b: number }\\n\\n// If no arguments are passed, the default type {} will not be useful,\\n// but syntactically it is allowed."],[1946,"Validating data on the client with schemas is the process of checking user input against predefined rules (schemas) before sending it to the server. Popular libraries such as Zod or Yup let you describe those rules declaratively. This gives the user instant feedback, reduces the load on the server and prevents incorrect data from being sent. The approach matters especially in registration and payment forms, or any input fields.","Validating data on the client is a critically important stage in web development that ensures the information entered by the user is correct before it is sent to the server. Using validation schemas lets you structure this process, making it more reliable and easier to maintain. A schema is a set of rules describing the expected structure of and constraints on the data (a string of a particular length, a number within a range, a required field, for example).\\n\\nPopular libraries for schema validation\\nThe most widespread tools are:\\n- Zod: a library for TypeScript with an emphasis on type safety and inferring types from schemas.\\n- Yup: a lightweight library, often used with Formik, with a simple and clear syntax.\\n- Joi (more often on the server, but it can be used on the client too): a powerful library with a rich set of validators.\\n\\nHow it is applied\\nValidation schemas are integrated into the form-handling process. When a field changes or the form is submitted, the data is checked against the schema. If validation fails, the user sees an error message next to the field. This gives instant feedback and prevents an incorrect request from being sent.\\n\\nA code example using Zod\\n\\nIn the example above `safeParse` returns an object that indicates success or failure safely, which is convenient for handling. The errors contain detailed information that can be shown to the user.\\n\\nThe advantages of the approach\\n\\n- A single source of truth: the validation rules are described in one place (the schema) rather than scattered through the code.\\n- Type safety (TypeScript): libraries like Zod generate TypeScript types from the schemas automatically, which rules out type mismatches.\\n- Reuse: the same schema can be used for validation on the client and, if needed, on the server.\\n- Better UX: the user sees the errors immediately without waiting for the server\'s response.\\n\\nConclusion: using validation schemas on the client is a modern, effective approach that is worth applying in every form where the correctness of the input matters. It is especially useful in complex forms with many fields and interrelated rules, since it makes the validation code predictable and easy to test.","import { z } from \'zod\';\\n\\n// 1. We define the schema for the registration form\'s data\\nconst userSchema = z.object({\\n  email: z.string().email(\'Invalid email\'),\\n  password: z.string().min(8, \'The password must be at least 8 characters\'),\\n  age: z.number().int().min(18, \'The age must be 18 or over\').optional(),\\n});\\n\\n// 2. The data entered by the user\\nconst userInput = {\\n  email: \'user@example\', // Error: an invalid email\\n  password: \'123\',        // Error: the password is too short\\n};\\n\\n// 3. The validation attempt\\nconst result = userSchema.safeParse(userInput);\\n\\nif (!result.success) {\\n  // 4. Handling the errors\\n  console.log(result.error.errors);\\n  // Prints an array of objects describing the errors for each field\\n  // This can be shown in the interface\\n} else {\\n  // The data is valid and can be sent to the server\\n  const validData = result.data;\\n  console.log(\'The data passed validation:\', validData);\\n}"],[1947,"A default export creates a single \\"main\\" entity for a module, which can lead to renaming problems on import and degrades automatic refactoring. The importing side can give it any name, which reduces the clarity and consistency of the code. It also complicates re-exporting and the analysis of dependencies by tooling.","A default export is ES6 module syntax that lets you export one \\"main\\" entity from a module. Although this seems convenient, the approach has several significant drawbacks that affect the maintainability and clarity of the code.\\n\\nProblems with renaming and clarity\\nWhen importing a default export, a developer can give it any name. This leads to inconsistency in the codebase, where the same component may be called differently in different files, which makes reading and searching harder.\\n\\nDifficulties with refactoring and analysis\\nStatic-analysis tools (an IDE\'s rename feature, for example) work less well with default exports, because the link between the export and the import is less explicit. Re-exporting a default export through barrel files (index.js) also requires special syntax and is less intuitive.\\n\\nProblems with the dependency tree\\nSome tools, such as Webpack, can apply more aggressive removal of unused code (tree shaking) to named exports, because they are listed explicitly. With a default export, which is often one large object, this can work less effectively.\\n\\nConclusion\\nUsing a default export is justified when a module really does represent a single logical entity — a class or a React component in its own file, for example — and you want to emphasize that. For libraries, utilities and large projects, however, named exports are preferable, since they improve clarity, simplify refactoring and encourage better tree shaking.","// module.js\\nexport default function MyComponent() {}\\n\\n// fileA.js\\nimport Component from \'./module\'; // The name \'Component\'\\n\\n// fileB.js\\nimport MySuperComponent from \'./module\'; // The name \'MySuperComponent\'\\n\\n// The re-export problem\\nexport { default as MyComponent } from \'./MyComponent\'; // Special syntax\\n// With a named export it would be simpler:\\n// export { MyComponent } from \'./MyComponent\';"],[1948,"Copying an object with JSON.stringify and JSON.parse creates a deep copy but loses all TypeScript type information. Every property becomes of type any or unknown unless you state it explicitly. Class methods, symbols, undefined values and circular references are also lost. It is a simple way of deep-copying simple data, but a dangerous one for complex objects with methods.","In TypeScript types exist only at compile time and are \\"erased\\" when the code runs. The `JSON.stringify()` and `JSON.parse()` methods work with values at runtime, taking no account of TypeScript\'s type system.\\n\\nWhat happens to the types?\\nAfter deserialization TypeScript sees the object as an ordinary object literal. If you do not provide an explicit type annotation or type assertion, the compiler will infer a wide type based on the structure of the JSON, which can lead to typing errors.\\n\\nA practical example and what is lost\\nConsider a class and copying it:\\n\\nHow do you preserve the typing?\\nSo that TypeScript understands the type correctly after copying, you need to state it explicitly:\\n- Use a type assertion: `const copy = JSON.parse(JSON.stringify(orig)) as User;` (careful, this can be unsafe).\\n- Create a constructor or factory method that takes an object and returns an instance of the class.\\n- Use serialization/deserialization libraries with type support (class-transformer, for example).\\n\\nConclusion\\nJSON copying is a quick way of deep-copying simple data objects (POJOs), but it ignores TypeScript\'s type system entirely and loses meta-information (methods, prototypes). Use it for data without behaviour, and for complex objects with methods apply other cloning methods or restore the types explicitly after deserialization.","class User {\\n  constructor(public name: string, public age: number) {}\\n  greet() { return `Hello, ${this.name}`; }\\n}\\n\\nconst original = new User(\'Alice\', 30);\\n// TypeScript knows that original is an instance of User\\n\\nconst jsonCopy: any = JSON.parse(JSON.stringify(original));\\n// jsonCopy is now a plain object { name: \'Alice\', age: 30 }\\n// Type: any (we stated it explicitly, but unknown could have been used)\\n// The greet() method is lost!\\n// console.log(jsonCopy.greet()); // Runtime error: greet is not a function\\n\\n// Without an annotation TypeScript will infer the type { name: string; age: number; }\\nconst inferredCopy = JSON.parse(JSON.stringify(original));\\n// inferredCopy has the type { name: string; age: number; }, not User."],[1949,"The Shared layer (or common layer) contains code that is used by several other layers of the application but does not belong directly to any of them. It usually holds shared utilities, constants, base types (DTOs, models), repository and service interfaces, and infrastructure-related code that does not depend on business logic. The main rule is that the Shared layer must not depend on outer layers such as the UI or the infrastructure, in order to avoid circular dependencies and ensure reuse.","In a layered architecture such as Clean Architecture or Onion Architecture, the Shared layer (sometimes called Core or Domain) plays a key role in organizing dependencies. It serves as a central hub containing the elements common to several layers, which lets you avoid code duplication and violations of the Dependency Inversion principle.\\n\\nWhat usually lives in the Shared layer?\\n\\n- Shared data models (DTOs, Entities, Value Objects): base classes or interfaces describing the structure of the data that is passed between layers.\\n- Repository and service interfaces: abstractions defining the contracts for working with data or external services. Their implementations will live in the infrastructure layer.\\n- Utilities and helpers: shared functions such as validators, parsers and mathematical calculations that do not depend on any particular business logic.\\n- Constants and enums: values common to the whole application — error codes, order statuses, for example.\\n- Cross-cutting concerns: base implementations for logging, exception handling and caching, provided they are abstracted away from specific frameworks.\\n\\nAn example structure and code\\nConsider a simple example in TypeScript for an e-commerce application. In the Shared layer we can define a repository interface and a base product model.\\n\\nRules and restrictions\\nIt is critically important that the Shared layer does not depend on outer layers such as the UI (React components, for example), the infrastructure (databases, HTTP clients) or frameworks. This prevents circular dependencies and makes the code more testable and reusable. For example, the `IProductRepository` interface is declared in Shared, while its implementation `MongoProductRepository` lives in the infrastructure layer, which depends on Shared.\\n\\nConclusion: the Shared layer should be used to hold code that is common and independent of specific implementation details. This increases consistency, reduces duplication and makes the application easier to maintain, especially on large projects with a clear separation of responsibilities.","// shared/interfaces/IProductRepository.ts\\nexport interface IProductRepository {\\n  findById(id: string): Promise<Product | null>;\\n  findAll(): Promise<Product[]>;\\n  save(product: Product): Promise<void>;\\n}\\n\\n// shared/models/Product.ts\\nexport class Product {\\n  constructor(\\n    public id: string,\\n    public name: string,\\n    public price: number,\\n    public category: string\\n  ) {}\\n\\n  // A shared method that can be used in different layers\\n  applyDiscount(percent: number): void {\\n    if (percent > 0 && percent <= 100) {\\n      this.price = this.price * (1 - percent / 100);\\n    }\\n  }\\n}\\n\\n// shared/utils/validation.ts\\nexport function isValidEmail(email: string): boolean {\\n  const regex = /^[^\\\\s@]+@[^\\\\s@]+\\\\.[^\\\\s@]+$/;\\n  return regex.test(email);\\n}"],[1950,"A ValueType stores its data directly in the variable, while a ReferenceType stores a reference to data in memory. Assigning a ValueType creates a copy of the value, whereas assigning a ReferenceType copies only the reference, and both variables then point at the same data. ValueTypes are usually placed on the stack, which makes operations on them fast, while ReferenceTypes are placed on the heap, which lets you work with large, dynamic data structures.","In programming languages such as C#, Java or TypeScript, all data types fall into two main categories: value types (ValueType) and reference types (ReferenceType). This division determines how data is stored in memory and how it is passed between variables and methods.\\n\\nStorage in memory\\nValue types store their data directly in the area of memory allocated for the variable. This is usually the stack — a fast, ordered area of memory managed automatically. Examples are the primitive types: int, bool, float, and also structures (struct in C#).\\n\\nReference types store in the variable not the data itself but the address (the reference) of the area of memory on the heap where that data lives. The heap is a dynamic area of memory managed by the garbage collector. Examples are classes, arrays and strings (in some languages).\\n\\nBehaviour on assignment and when passing\\nThe key difference shows up when copying:\\n- When a value-type variable is assigned to another variable, the value itself is copied. After that the two variables are completely independent.\\n- When a reference-type variable is assigned, only the reference (the address) is copied. Both variables then point at the same object on the heap. Changes made through one variable will be visible through the other.\\n\\nAn example in C#\\n\\nWhere it is applied\\nValue types are ideal for small, frequently used data where performance and copy semantics matter (the coordinates of a point, sums of money, for example). Reference types are used for large objects that can change dynamically and should be passed by reference for efficiency (lists of users, an application\'s configuration, for example).\\n\\nConclusion: use value types for simple, indivisible data where independent copying is needed. Use reference types for complex objects where shared state and efficient memory management are necessary.","// A ValueType example (a structure)\\nint a = 10;\\nint b = a; // The value 10 is copied\\nb = 20;\\nConsole.WriteLine(a); // Output: 10 (a has not changed)\\n\\n// A ReferenceType example (a class)\\nclass Person { public string Name; }\\nPerson p1 = new Person { Name = \\"Alice\\" };\\nPerson p2 = p1; // The reference is copied, p2 points at the same object\\np2.Name = \\"Bob\\";\\nConsole.WriteLine(p1.Name); // Output: Bob (the object was changed through p2)"],[1951,"Generics let you create components that work with different types while preserving type information. Using object erases that information, making the code less type-safe. With generics the compiler can check types at compile time, preventing errors. It also improves autocompletion in the IDE and the documentation of the code. Generics make code more expressive and reusable without losing type safety.","Generics are a mechanism that lets you create functions, classes or interfaces that can work with various data types while preserving information about the concrete type. The key difference from using the `object` type lies in preserving type safety and type information throughout the life cycle of the code.\\n\\nThe problem with using object\\nThe `object` type in TypeScript represents any non-primitive value (everything except number, string, boolean, symbol, null and undefined). When a function accepts or returns `object`, the information about that object\'s concrete structure is lost. This forces you to use type assertions or runtime checks, which weakens static type checking.\\n\\nThe solution with generics\\nGenerics let you parameterize the type, creating a \\"template\\" that will be filled in with a concrete type at the call site. The compiler tracks that type, providing compile-time checking.\\n\\nThe key advantages of generics over object\\n\\n- Type safety: the compiler prevents operations on incompatible types, reducing runtime errors.\\n- Preserved type information: no type casting is required, and the code becomes cleaner and more reliable.\\n- Reusability: one generic component can work with many types, avoiding code duplication.\\n- Better IDE support: development tools provide accurate autocompletion and hints based on the concrete type.\\n- Stricter contracts: you can impose constraints on generic parameters (`T extends SomeInterface`, for example), guaranteeing that particular properties or methods are present.\\n\\nPractical use\\nGenerics are widely used in libraries and frameworks. For example, in React for typing component props and state, in data structures (maps, sets), when working with promises (`Promise<T>`) or in utility functions such as `pick` and `omit`.\\n\\nConclusion: generics should be applied whenever you need to create a reusable component that has to work with different types while preserving full type safety. Using `object` is justified only in rare cases where the type really is unknown or unimportant (when working with untyped external libraries, for example), and even then `unknown` is often the better choice.","// An example with object: type safety is lost\\nfunction getFirstElement(arr: object[]): object {\\n    return arr[0];\\n}\\nconst numbers = [1, 2, 3];\\nconst first = getFirstElement(numbers); // The type of first is object\\n// console.log(first.toFixed(1)); // Compile error: the property toFixed does not exist on the type object\\n// A type cast is required: (first as number).toFixed(1)\\n\\n// The same example with generics\\nfunction getFirstElementGeneric<T>(arr: T[]): T {\\n    return arr[0];\\n}\\nconst numbers = [1, 2, 3];\\nconst firstNum = getFirstElementGeneric(numbers); // The type of firstNum is inferred as number\\nconsole.log(firstNum.toFixed(1)); // OK, the compiler knows this is a number\\n\\nconst strings = [\\"a\\", \\"b\\", \\"c\\"];\\nconst firstStr = getFirstElementGeneric(strings); // The type of firstStr is string\\nconsole.log(firstStr.toUpperCase()); // OK\\n\\n// An example with a constraint\\ninterface HasId {\\n    id: number;\\n}\\nfunction getById<T extends HasId>(items: T[], id: number): T | undefined {\\n    return items.find(item => item.id === id);\\n}\\n// The compiler guarantees that the array\'s elements have an id property."],[1952,"Null represents the absence of a value, but using it often leads to runtime errors such as NullPointerException. It violates the principles of type safety, because a variable can be either an object or null. The code becomes overloaded with null checks, which makes it harder to read and maintain. Some languages, Kotlin for example, have introduced nullable types to solve this problem.","The concept of null was introduced in programming to denote the absence of a value for a reference or pointer. Although this seems convenient, its widespread use gives rise to a number of serious problems that can crash applications and make debugging difficult.\\n\\nThe main problems with null\\n\\n- Runtime errors (NullPointerException/NPE): the most frequent problem is trying to call a method or access a property on a null reference, which crashes the program immediately.\\n- Violation of type safety: if a language lets any reference be null, then the type system cannot guarantee that a variable contains a valid object, which weakens static analysis.\\n- Code polluted with checks: developers are forced to add checks constantly (if (obj != null)), which increases the volume of code, makes it less readable and can mask logical errors.\\n- Ambiguous meaning: null can mean different things — \\"the value was not found\\", \\"the value has not been initialized yet\\" or \\"the value is deliberately absent\\" — which makes code harder to understand.\\n\\nA code example in Java\\n\\nApproaches to a solution\\nModern languages and paradigms offer alternatives. For example, using the Optional pattern (Java, TypeScript) or built-in nullable types (Kotlin, TypeScript with strictNullChecks). These approaches force the developer to handle the case of a missing value explicitly at the type level, which prevents many errors.\\n\\nConclusion: problems with null are a classic source of errors. Using language features (Optional, nullable types) or strict code conventions helps make a program more predictable and robust. Knowing about these problems matters especially when designing APIs and the public methods of libraries.","public class UserService {\\n    public String getUserName(User user) {\\n        // A potential problem: if user == null, the getProfile() call will fail.\\n        Profile profile = user.getProfile();\\n        return profile.getName(); // And here too, if profile == null.\\n    }\\n    // Safer but cluttered code:\\n    public String getUserNameSafe(User user) {\\n        if (user != null) {\\n            Profile profile = user.getProfile();\\n            if (profile != null) {\\n                return profile.getName();\\n            }\\n        }\\n        return \\"Unknown\\";\\n    }\\n}"],[1953,"Swagger (OpenAPI) is a tool for describing, documenting and testing RESTful APIs. By itself it is not used for the actual communication between services, but it provides an API specification from which client code, server stubs and documentation can be generated automatically. That specification serves as a contract that makes integrating services easier. Developers use the generated code for API calls, which makes the interaction more reliable and less error-prone.","Swagger, now more often called OpenAPI, is a specification for describing RESTful APIs in JSON or YAML format. Its main purpose is to create a machine-readable API contract that describes in detail all the endpoints, request parameters, response formats, data models and authentication methods.\\n\\nHow does Swagger help with communication between services?\\nAlthough Swagger is not itself a transport protocol (like HTTP or gRPC), it plays a key role in inter-service communication in a microservice architecture. The OpenAPI specification serves as the single source of truth for an API. When service A has to call service B, service B\'s team provides an up-to-date OpenAPI specification. Service A\'s team can use that specification to generate client code automatically in the programming language they need. The generated code encapsulates all the details of HTTP requests and of serializing and deserializing data, which simplifies and speeds up integration considerably.\\n\\nA practical example of use\\nConsider a scenario where a user service (`UserService`) provides an API for getting information about a user. Its OpenAPI specification might look like this:\\n\\nAn order service (`OrderService`) that needs the user\'s data can use a tool such as `openapi-generator` to create a client for `UserService` in TypeScript:\\n\\nThis approach removes the need to write HTTP clients and JSON parsing by hand, reducing the chance of errors caused by mismatched types or formats.\\n\\nWhere else is OpenAPI applied?\\n\\n - Documentation: tools such as Swagger UI or ReDoc create interactive documentation from the specification automatically.\\n - Mocking and testing: from the specification you can generate a mock server for testing clients before the backend is implemented.\\n - Validation: middleware (in Express.js, for example) can use the specification to validate incoming requests.\\n - Server code generation: you can generate the skeleton of the server code (controllers, models), which speeds development up.\\n\\nConclusion: Swagger (OpenAPI) is not a transport mechanism for communication, but it is critically important as a design and automation tool. It is worth applying in projects with several services in order to ensure clear contracts, reduce manual work and increase the reliability of integrations.","openapi: 3.0.3\\ninfo:\\n  title: User Service API\\n  version: 1.0.0\\npaths:\\n  /users/{userId}:\\n    get:\\n      summary: Get user by ID\\n      parameters:\\n        - name: userId\\n          in: path\\n          required: true\\n          schema:\\n            type: integer\\n      responses:\\n        \'200\':\\n          description: OK\\n          content:\\n            application/json:\\n              schema:\\n                $ref: \'#/components/schemas/User\'\\ncomponents:\\n  schemas:\\n    User:\\n      type: object\\n      properties:\\n        id:\\n          type: integer\\n        name:\\n          type: string\\n        email:\\n          type: string\\n\\n// The generated client code (an example)\\nimport { UserApi } from \'./generated-client\';\\n\\nconst userApi = new UserApi();\\nasync function getOrderWithUser(orderId: number, userId: number) {\\n  try {\\n    const user = await userApi.getUserById(userId); // A call through the generated client\\n    console.log(`Order ${orderId} belongs to ${user.name}`);\\n  } catch (error) {\\n    console.error(\'Failed to fetch user:\', error);\\n  }\\n}"],[1954,"In the classic understanding an interface contains only method signatures without their implementations. In modern languages such as Java (from version 8) and C# (from 8.0), however, method implementations can be added to interfaces using the \'default\' keyword (Java) or with modifiers (C#). This lets you extend interfaces by adding new functionality without breaking the existing code of the classes that implement them. The approach helps APIs evolve and reduces the need for abstract classes that exist only to provide a shared implementation.","Traditionally an interface in object-oriented programming defines a contract — the set of methods a class must implement — without providing any implementation. This provides polymorphism and abstraction, letting different classes share a common interface but have different behaviour.\\n\\nThe evolution of interfaces\\nAs programming languages developed, a need for more flexible constructs appeared. For example, when a new method was added to an interface, every existing class implementing it would have to provide an implementation, which broke backward compatibility. To solve this problem, default methods were introduced in Java 8, and in C# 8.0 methods with an implementation in interfaces (using modifiers such as an explicit `virtual` and `override`).\\n\\nHow it works\\nA default method in an interface has a body and is marked with the `default` keyword (in Java) or can be declared as `virtual` with an implementation (in C#). A class implementing the interface can use that default implementation or override it if different behaviour is required.\\n\\nPractical use\\nThis capability is especially useful when developing libraries and frameworks, where interfaces can evolve without breaking existing code. For example, in the Java Collections Framework the `stream()` and `forEach()` methods were added to the collection interfaces as default methods, which made it possible to introduce functional capabilities without requiring changes in all the implementing classes.\\n\\nIt is important, however, not to abuse this capability and turn interfaces into something resembling abstract classes. Interfaces should still define a contract first and foremost, and any shared logic should be minimal and non-functional (helper methods, for example).\\n\\nConclusion: adding method implementations to interfaces is a powerful tool for ensuring backward compatibility and improving an API gradually. It is worth applying when you need to extend an interface\'s functionality without breaking existing implementations, but the main business logic is better left in classes.","// An example in Java\\ninterface Vehicle {\\n    void start(); // An abstract method\\n    \\n    default void honk() { // A default method\\n        System.out.println(\\"Beep beep!\\");\\n    }\\n}\\n\\nclass Car implements Vehicle {\\n    @Override\\n    public void start() {\\n        System.out.println(\\"Car started\\");\\n    }\\n    // The honk() method is inherited with its default implementation\\n}"],[1955,"Dependency injection (DI) is a design pattern in which an object\'s dependencies (services or other objects, for example) are supplied to it from outside rather than created inside the object itself. This makes code more flexible and easy to test, because the dependencies can be replaced with mocks. DI is often implemented through the constructor, through properties or through methods. It is a key part of many modern frameworks such as Angular, Spring and NestJS.","Dependency injection (DI) is a design pattern that implements the principle of Inversion of Control (IoC). Its core idea is that an object should not create its own dependencies but receive them ready-made from outside. This separates the creation of objects from their use, which makes the system more modular and easier to test.\\n\\nHow it works\\nInstead of a class creating instances of the services it needs, those dependencies are \\"injected\\" into it through the constructor, through properties (setters) or through methods. Constructor injection is the most widespread and the recommended way, since it makes the dependencies explicit and guarantees that the object is created in a valid state.\\n\\nAn example without DI and with DI\\nConsider an example in TypeScript. Without DI the `UserService` class creates the repository instance itself, which creates tight coupling and complicates testing.\\n\\nWith DI the dependency is supplied from outside:\\n\\nDI containers\\nIn large applications creating and passing dependencies by hand becomes cumbersome. DI containers (or IoC containers) are used to automate this process. The container knows how to create objects and resolve their dependencies, often from configuration or metadata (decorators in TypeScript, for example).\\n\\nWhere and why it is applied\\n\\n - Testing: it is easy to substitute a real dependency (a database, for example) with a mock or stub.\\n - Flexibility and maintenance: replacing a dependency\'s implementation (changing the library for sending email, for example) requires changes in only one place — the container\'s configuration.\\n - Following the SOLID principles: DI directly supports the Dependency Inversion Principle.\\n - Architecture: it is the foundation of many modern frameworks (Angular, Spring, ASP.NET Core, NestJS).\\n\\nConclusion: dependency injection is worth applying in projects of medium and large complexity where testability, architectural flexibility and ease of maintenance matter. It is especially useful in server applications and large client-side SPAs.","// WITHOUT DI: bad practice\\nclass UserService {\\n    private userRepository: UserRepository;\\n    constructor() {\\n        this.userRepository = new UserRepository(); // The dependency is created inside\\n    }\\n    getUser(id: number) {\\n        return this.userRepository.find(id);\\n    }\\n}\\n\\n// WITH DI: good practice\\nclass UserService {\\n    constructor(private userRepository: UserRepository) {}\\n    // The dependency is injected through the constructor\\n    getUser(id: number) {\\n        return this.userRepository.find(id);\\n    }\\n}\\n\\n// Somewhere in the application\'s code we create the dependencies and \\"assemble\\" the object\\nconst repo = new UserRepository();\\nconst service = new UserService(repo); // We inject the dependency\\n\\n// An example of using the Injectable decorator in NestJS\\nimport { Injectable } from \'@nestjs/common\';\\nimport { UserRepository } from \'./user.repository\';\\n\\n@Injectable() // The decorator marks the class as managed by the container\\nclass UserService {\\n    constructor(private userRepository: UserRepository) {}\\n}\\n\\n// The NestJS container will create UserRepository automatically and inject it into UserService."],[1956,"The Strategy pattern should be applied when you have several related algorithms or variants of behaviour and need to choose between them while the program runs. It is useful when a class contains a lot of conditional statements for selecting behaviour. The pattern encapsulates each algorithm in its own class, making them interchangeable. This makes it easy to add new algorithms without changing the existing client code. Strategy helps you observe SOLID principles such as open/closed and single responsibility.","The Strategy pattern is a behavioural design pattern that defines a family of algorithms, encapsulates each of them and makes them interchangeable. This lets you choose the algorithm you need while the program is running rather than at compile time.\\n\\nThe core idea and its uses\\nThe main goal is to separate the algorithm from the object that uses it. This is especially useful in systems where an object\'s behaviour has to change dynamically depending on the context or on user input. For example, a navigation application may use different route-building algorithms (walking, driving, public transport) that can be switched at the user\'s wish.\\n\\nA practical example\\nConsider a simple example of a payment system that supports different methods (CreditCard, PayPal, Crypto). Instead of piling conditional statements into the order class, we factor out strategies.\\n\\nThe key advantages\\n\\n- Getting rid of conditional statements: it removes long switch/case or if/else blocks.\\n- Flexibility and extensibility: new algorithms are added easily without modifying the existing context code.\\n- Observing SOLID: each strategy has a single responsibility, and the context is open for extension.\\n- Testability: strategies can be tested in isolation.\\n\\nConclusion: the Strategy pattern is worth applying when a system contains several variations of one algorithm or behaviour that can change depending on external conditions, and you want to keep the code clean, flexible and easy to maintain. It is a perfect fit for scenarios where rules or logic are added or changed often.","// The strategy interface\\ninterface PaymentStrategy {\\n    pay(amount: number): void;\\n}\\n\\n// Concrete strategies\\nclass CreditCardPayment implements PaymentStrategy {\\n    pay(amount: number) {\\n        console.log(`Paying ${amount} using Credit Card.`);\\n        // Card processing logic\\n    }\\n}\\n\\nclass PayPalPayment implements PaymentStrategy {\\n    pay(amount: number) {\\n        console.log(`Paying ${amount} using PayPal.`);\\n        // PayPal API logic\\n    }\\n}\\n\\n// The context that uses the strategy\\nclass ShoppingCart {\\n    private strategy: PaymentStrategy;\\n\\n    constructor(strategy: PaymentStrategy) {\\n        this.strategy = strategy;\\n    }\\n\\n    setPaymentStrategy(strategy: PaymentStrategy) {\\n        this.strategy = strategy; // Changing the strategy dynamically\\n    }\\n\\n    checkout(amount: number) {\\n        this.strategy.pay(amount);\\n    }\\n}\\n\\n// Use\\nconst cart = new ShoppingCart(new CreditCardPayment());\\ncart.checkout(100); // Paying 100 using Credit Card.\\n\\ncart.setPaymentStrategy(new PayPalPayment());\\ncart.checkout(200); // Paying 200 using PayPal."],[1957,"Missing keys in data are a frequent problem, especially when working with APIs or user input. The main approaches include checking whether a key exists before accessing it, using the optional chaining (?.) and nullish coalescing (??) operators in JavaScript/TypeScript, and setting default values. It is also important to validate data at an early stage so as to avoid errors deep inside the code. These methods make code more reliable and predictable.","When receiving data from external sources such as an API, configuration files or user forms, you can never be completely sure of its structure. A key you expect to find on an object may be absent, which leads to an error of the kind `Cannot read property \'...\' of undefined` and possibly to the application crashing. Handling missing keys is therefore a fundamental skill for building robust software.\\n\\nThe main handling strategies\\nThere are several widespread approaches, which can be combined:\\n- Checking whether a key exists: an explicit check with a condition before accessing nested properties.\\n- Optional chaining (?.): a modern operator that lets you access the properties of deeply nested objects safely without raising an error if some intermediate property equals `null` or `undefined`.\\n- Nullish coalescing (??): an operator that returns the right-hand operand only if the left-hand one equals `null` or `undefined`. It is ideal for setting default values.\\n- Default parameters: in functions you can set default values for parameters, which is especially useful when destructuring objects.\\n- Validating data on input: using validation libraries (Joi, Yup, Zod, for example) or writing your own checks to make sure the data matches the expected schema before using it in business logic.\\n\\nCode examples in JavaScript\\nConsider a user object that may arrive from an API:\\n\\nWhere this is applied\\nThese techniques are universal and used everywhere data is involved: frontend applications handling server responses; backend services parsing incoming HTTP requests; scripts that process configuration files. They matter especially in a microservice architecture, where services talk to one another and the format of the data can change.\\n\\nConclusion: handling missing keys is a mandatory practice for writing error-resistant code. Use optional chaining and default values for brevity and readability, and for critically important data supplement them with strict validation at the boundaries of the system.","// The input data: the object may be incomplete\\nconst apiResponse = {\\n    id: 123,\\n    // The \'profile\' property may be absent\\n};\\n\\n// 1. The classic check (verbose but reliable)\\nlet userName1 = \'Guest\';\\nif (apiResponse.profile && apiResponse.profile.name) {\\n    userName1 = apiResponse.profile.name;\\n}\\n\\n// 2. Using optional chaining and nullish coalescing (the modern way)\\nconst userName2 = apiResponse?.profile?.name ?? \'Guest\';\\n\\n// 3. Destructuring with default parameters\\nfunction getUserInfo({ profile = {} } = {}) {\\n    const { name = \'Guest\' } = profile;\\n    return name;\\n}\\nconst userName3 = getUserInfo(apiResponse);\\n\\nconsole.log(userName1, userName2, userName3); // All will print \'Guest\'"],[1958,"The compiler considers an interface functional if it contains exactly one abstract method (a Single Abstract Method — SAM). Such an interface can be implemented with a lambda expression or a method reference. The @FunctionalInterface annotation helps the compiler check this condition at compile time, but it is not required in order to create a functional interface.","In languages that support functional programming (Java from version 8, for example) the key notion is the functional interface. This is an interface that contains exactly one abstract method. It is precisely the presence of only one abstract method that lets the compiler \\"understand\\" that this interface can be implemented with a compact lambda expression rather than through a cumbersome anonymous class.\\n\\nThe criteria for a functional interface\\nThe compiler checks the following conditions:\\n- The interface must contain exactly one abstract method (a method without an implementation).\\n- The interface may, however, contain any number of default methods with an implementation.\\n- The interface may contain any number of static methods.\\n- Methods inherited from the `Object` class (`toString`, `equals`, for example) are not counted as abstract.\\n\\nThe role of the @FunctionalInterface annotation\\nThe `@FunctionalInterface` annotation is optional but extremely useful. It serves two main functions:\\n- A compile-time check: if a developer marks an interface with this annotation but the interface does not satisfy the SAM criterion, the compiler reports an error. This prevents accidental changes that could break the functionality of lambda expressions.\\n- Documenting the code: the annotation indicates the interface\'s purpose explicitly to other developers.\\n\\nCode examples\\nConsider an example of a correct functional interface:\\n\\nAn example of an interface that is NOT functional (it will cause a compile error with the annotation):\\n\\nWhere functional interfaces are applied\\nFunctional interfaces are widely used in modern Java code:\\n- In the Streams API for the `filter`, `map` and `reduce` operations.\\n- In concurrency with `ExecutorService` and `CompletableFuture`.\\n- In event handling and in libraries such as JavaFX.\\n- To create clean, declarative and reusable blocks of behaviour.\\n\\nConclusion: a functional interface should be used when you need to define a single action or predicate that will often be implemented through lambda expressions. It is the basis for writing concise, expressive code in the functional programming paradigm in Java.","// The annotation helps the compiler check the SAM condition\\n@FunctionalInterface\\ninterface StringProcessor {\\n    // The single abstract method\\n    String process(String input);\\n    \\n    // A default method — allowed\\n    default void log(String msg) {\\n        System.out.println(\\"LOG: \\" + msg);\\n    }\\n    \\n    // A static method — allowed\\n    static boolean isEmpty(String s) {\\n        return s == null || s.trim().isEmpty();\\n    }\\n}\\n\\n// Use with a lambda\\nStringProcessor toUpper = (str) -> str.toUpperCase();\\nSystem.out.println(toUpper.process(\\"hello\\")); // Prints: HELLO\\n\\n@FunctionalInterface // COMPILE ERROR: several abstract methods\\ninterface InvalidProcessor {\\n    void methodA();\\n    void methodB(); // The second abstract method violates the SAM condition\\n}"],[1959,"Reactive programming is a paradigm oriented towards working with streams of data and the automatic propagation of change. Instead of explicitly requesting new data, you declare how the system should react when it arrives. The key concepts are the Observable (an observable stream), the Observer and the operators for transforming them. It is especially useful for handling events, HTTP requests or user input, where data arrives asynchronously.","Reactive programming is a style of programming in which the main focus is on streams of data and the automatic propagation of change. Instead of the imperative approach, where you tell the computer how to do things step by step, you describe declaratively the relationships between the various sources of data (the streams) and how the application should react to them.\\n\\nThe key concepts\\nThe main building blocks are:\\n- Observable: represents a stream of data or events that can be emitted over time. This may be a single value, many values or even an error.\\n- Observer: an object that subscribes to an Observable and reacts to the data that arrives (next), to errors (error) or to the signal that the stream has finished (complete).\\n- Operators: pure functions (such as map, filter and merge) that let you transform, combine and manipulate streams, creating new Observables.\\n\\nWhere is this applied?\\nThis paradigm is a perfect fit for scenarios with asynchronous, event-driven data:\\n- Handling user events (clicks, keyboard input).\\n- Working with HTTP requests and WebSocket messages.\\n- Managing state in complex frontend applications.\\n- Processing data in real time, in dashboards or chats for example.\\n\\nA code example (RxJS)\\nConsider a simple example of handling user input with a delay (debounce) for a search.\\n\\nIn this example we created a reactive stream out of input events. The `debounceTime` and `distinctUntilChanged` operators help optimize performance by reducing the number of useless search operations.\\n\\nConclusion\\nReactive programming is worth applying when your application relies heavily on asynchronous events or streams of data. It makes code more declarative and composable and simplifies the handling of complex scenarios such as cancelling requests, merging streams or handling errors. For simple synchronous tasks, however, it can add unnecessary complexity.","import { fromEvent } from \'rxjs\';\\nimport { debounceTime, map, distinctUntilChanged } from \'rxjs/operators\';\\n\\n// We get a reference to the input field\\nconst searchInput = document.getElementById(\'search\');\\n\\n// We create an Observable out of the input events\\nconst searchStream = fromEvent(searchInput, \'input\').pipe(\\n  // We turn the event into the field\'s value\\n  map(event => event.target.value),\\n  // We wait 300 ms after the last input\\n  debounceTime(300),\\n  // We ignore consecutive repeated values\\n  distinctUntilChanged()\\n);\\n\\n// We subscribe to the stream\\nconst subscription = searchStream.subscribe(searchTerm => {\\n  console.log(\'Searching for:\', searchTerm);\\n  // Here an HTTP request could be sent\\n});\\n\\n// Later you can unsubscribe: subscription.unsubscribe();"],[1960,"Utility Types are generic types built into TypeScript that simplify transforming existing types. They let you create new types from ones you already have — for example, making all fields optional (Partial) or selecting only the ones you need (Pick). This reduces code duplication and improves readability.","What are Utility Types?\\nUtility Types are a set of generic types built into TypeScript that let you transform existing types easily and flexibly. They spare you from describing a new interface by hand for every variation of your data, which is especially useful when working with APIs, forms or application state.\\n\\nThe main Utility Types\\n\\n- Partial<T> — makes all properties of the type T optional.\\n- Required<T> — makes all properties required.\\n- Readonly<T> — makes all properties read-only.\\n- Pick<T, K> — selects only the given keys K from T.\\n- Omit<T, K> — excludes the given keys K from T.\\n- Record<K, T> — creates an object type with the keys K and values of type T.\\n\\nAn example of use\\n\\nWhere they are applied\\nUtility Types are widely used in real projects: for describing partial updates (PATCH requests), creating simplified representations of data (for lists, for example), working with Redux reducers or with component state. They help preserve type safety without excessive code duplication.\\n\\nConclusion: Utility Types are a powerful TypeScript tool for flexible work with types. They are worth applying wherever you need to create a variation of an existing type without writing a new interface, which speeds up development and reduces the number of errors.","interface User {\\n  id: number;\\n  name: string;\\n  email: string;\\n}\\n\\n// A partial update of a user\\nfunction updateUser(id: number, updates: Partial<User>) {\\n  // updates may contain only name, only email or both\\n}\\n\\n// Selecting only the name and email for display\\ntype UserPreview = Pick<User, \'name\' | \'email\'>;\\n\\n// Excluding id from the form\'s data\\ntype UserForm = Omit<User, \'id\'>;"],[1961,"Utility Types are generic types built into TypeScript that simplify manipulating types. For example, Pick creates a type with the selected keys from T, Omit excludes the given keys, and Partial makes all fields optional. They help avoid code duplication and make types more flexible.","What are Utility Types?\\nUtility Types are a set of generic types built into TypeScript that let you create new types from existing ones easily. They solve typical type-transformation tasks without any need to write complex conditional constructs by hand.\\n\\nThe main Utility Types\\n\\n- Pick<T, K> — selects from the type T only those properties whose keys are listed in K. Useful when you need to take a subset of an object\'s fields.\\n- Omit<T, K> — excludes from the type T the properties with keys from K. Convenient for removing fields, for example when creating a type without an identifier.\\n- Partial<T> — makes all properties of the type T optional. Often used for updating part of an object.\\n- Required<T> — makes all properties required, the opposite of Partial.\\n- Readonly<T> — makes all properties read-only.\\n- Record<K, T> — creates an object type with keys from K and values of type T.\\n\\nExamples of use\\n\\nWhere they are applied\\nUtility Types are used actively in real projects for creating the types of API responses, forms, Redux state and component props. For example, when working with forms Partial is often applied to the form data\'s type, and when filtering fields — Pick or Omit.\\n\\nConclusion: Utility Types are an indispensable tool for writing clean, safe TypeScript code. They are worth applying wherever flexible work with types is required, in order to avoid duplication and improve readability.","interface User {\\n  id: number;\\n  name: string;\\n  email: string;\\n  age: number;\\n}\\n\\n// Pick: only the name and email\\ntype UserContact = Pick<User, \'name\' | \'email\'>;\\n\\n// Omit: everything except id\\ntype UserWithoutId = Omit<User, \'id\'>;\\n\\n// Partial: all fields optional for an update\\ntype UpdateUser = Partial<User>;\\n\\n// Record: a dictionary of users by id\\ntype UserMap = Record<number, User>;"],[1962,"Optional fields in TypeScript are marked with a ? and may contain the value undefined. The main difficulty is the need to check that a value is present before using it, otherwise TypeScript reports an error. This helps avoid runtime errors but requires additional checks or the use of operators such as optional chaining.","What are optional fields?\\nIn TypeScript optional fields are an object\'s properties that may be absent or equal to undefined. They are marked with a question mark after the field\'s name, for example: `name?: string`. This means the field\'s type is `string | undefined`, but with additional behaviour: when accessing such a field TypeScript requires a check for undefined.\\n\\nThe main difficulties\\n\\n- The need for a check: when accessing an optional field without a check TypeScript reports an error if strictNullChecks is enabled. For example, `obj.name.toUpperCase()` will cause an error, because name may be undefined.\\n- Optional chaining: to simplify this work the `?.` operator is used, which lets you access nested properties safely: `obj?.name?.toUpperCase()`.\\n- Typing functions: when passing optional fields into functions you need to bear in mind that they may be undefined and handle that accordingly.\\n\\nA code example\\n\\nConclusion\\nOptional fields are useful for describing properties that are not required, but they require careful handling of undefined. Use optional chaining and default values to avoid errors and make your code safer.","interface User {\\n  name?: string;\\n  age: number;\\n}\\n\\nfunction greet(user: User) {\\n  // Error: Object is possibly \'undefined\'\\n  // console.log(user.name.toUpperCase());\\n  \\n  // Correct: a check or optional chaining\\n  console.log(user.name?.toUpperCase() ?? \'Anonymous\');\\n}\\n\\ngreet({ age: 25 }); // Prints \'Anonymous\'\\ngreet({ name: \'Alice\', age: 30 }); // Prints \'ALICE\'"],[1963,"In TypeScript you can obtain the keys of an object whose values have a particular type by using conditional types and key remapping. You create a type that iterates over the keys and checks whether the type of the value matches the one you want. If it does, the key stays; otherwise it is excluded. This lets you type functions that work with a subset of an object\'s properties strictly.","The concept of filtering keys by the type of their values\\nTypeScript provides powerful tools for manipulating types at the type-system level. One common task is to obtain only those keys of an object whose values have a particular type — only strings, for example. This is achieved with conditional types and the key-remapping syntax in mapped types.\\n\\nHow it works\\nThe core idea is to create a type that iterates over all the keys of the source object and checks whether the type of the value at that key is the one required. If it is, the key is included in the result; otherwise it is discarded. The `as` construct in a mapped type is used for this, which lets you transform keys.\\n\\nIn this example the type `KeysOfType` takes two parameters: `T`, the source object, and `V`, the value type you are looking for. Inside the mapped type we use `as` to include the key conditionally: if `T[K] extends V`, the key stays, otherwise it becomes `never`. We then cast the result to a type and extract its keys with `keyof`.\\n\\nPractical use\\nThis approach is useful for creating strictly typed functions that work only with particular properties of an object. For example, a function that extracts all the string values from an object:\\n\\nThe return type guarantees that the function will return only those properties that have a string type.\\n\\nConclusion\\nFiltering keys by the type of their values is a powerful TypeScript tool for creating type-safe utilities. It is especially useful when working with dynamic data where strict type checking at compile time is required, which reduces the chance of errors at runtime.","type KeysOfType<T, V> = {\\n  [K in keyof T as T[K] extends V ? K : never]: T[K]\\n} extends infer R ? keyof R : never;\\n\\n// An example of use\\ninterface User {\\n  name: string;\\n  age: number;\\n  email: string;\\n  isAdmin: boolean;\\n}\\n\\ntype StringKeys = KeysOfType<User, string>; // \\"name\\" | \\"email\\"\\n\\nfunction getStringValues<T extends Record<string, any>>(obj: T): Pick<T, KeysOfType<T, string>> {\\n  const result: any = {};\\n  for (const key in obj) {\\n    if (typeof obj[key] === \'string\') {\\n      result[key] = obj[key];\\n    }\\n  }\\n  return result;\\n}\\n\\nconst user: User = { name: \\"Alice\\", age: 30, email: \\"alice@example.com\\", isAdmin: false };\\nconst strings = getStringValues(user); // { name: \\"Alice\\", email: \\"alice@example.com\\" }"],[1964,"The construct as unknown as Type lets you bypass TypeScript\'s type system, which can lead to runtime errors. It erases type information and makes code less predictable. Instead you should use proper type checks or assertions with type narrowing.","Why as unknown as Type is bad practice\\nThe `as unknown as Type` construct in TypeScript lets you force any value to any type, ignoring the type system entirely. It is like using `any`, but with a false sense of safety, since the resulting type is stated explicitly.\\n\\nHow it works\\nFirst the value is cast to `unknown` — the safest type, which forbids any operation. Then it is immediately cast to the required type `Type`. The compiler does not check whether the source and target types are compatible, so the error may only show up at runtime.\\n\\nWhen it may be justified\\nIn rare cases — for example, when working with external data whose type is guaranteed to be known but cannot be expressed in TypeScript. Even then, however, it is better to use schema validation (Zod or io-ts, for example).\\n\\nConclusion\\nAvoid `as unknown as Type` in production code. Always aim at checking types at runtime, or use assertions with type narrowing, in order to preserve safety and readability.","const data: unknown = JSON.parse(\'{\\"name\\": \\"Alice\\"}\');\\n\\n// A dangerous cast\\nconst user = data as unknown as { name: string; age: number };\\nconsole.log(user.age); // undefined, but TypeScript thinks this is a number\\n\\n// The correct approach — a check\\nif (typeof data === \'object\' && data !== null && \'name\' in data) {\\n  const safeUser = data as { name: string };\\n  console.log(safeUser.name);\\n}"],[1965,"The unknown type in TypeScript represents a value whose type is not known. To handle an error of type unknown you first have to narrow its type with checks such as instanceof or typeof. This guarantees safe access to the error\'s properties. Without narrowing, the compiler will not let you perform operations on an unknown.","What unknown is and what it is for\\nIn TypeScript the `unknown` type is a type-safe alternative to `any`. It represents a value whose type is not known at the time the code is written. Unlike `any`, you cannot perform arbitrary operations on an `unknown` without first checking its type. This is especially useful when handling errors in a `catch` block, where the error has the type `unknown` by default (from TypeScript 4.0).\\n\\nHow do you handle an unknown error?\\nTo work with an `unknown` you need to narrow its type. The most widespread way is to use an `instanceof Error` check. If the error is an instance of the `Error` class, you can safely access the `message`, `name` and `stack` properties. If the error\'s type is unknown, you can check whether it is an object with a `message` field.\\n\\nPractical recommendations\\n\\n- Always use `unknown` rather than `any` in a `catch` block, for better type safety.\\n- Create a custom guard function to check the error\'s structure if it has a complex shape.\\n- Avoid casting through `as any` — it reduces safety.\\n\\nConclusion\\nHandling `unknown` errors in TypeScript requires explicit type narrowing, which makes code more predictable and protects against runtime errors. This approach is recommended in every TypeScript project for handling exceptions.","try {\\n  // code that may throw an error\\n} catch (error: unknown) {\\n  if (error instanceof Error) {\\n    console.log(error.message);\\n  } else if (typeof error === \'string\') {\\n    console.log(error);\\n  } else if (error && typeof error === \'object\' && \'message\' in error) {\\n    console.log((error as { message: string }).message);\\n  } else {\\n    console.log(\'Unknown error\');\\n  }\\n}"],[1966,"After TypeScript is compiled to JavaScript the \\"+\\" operator behaves like the ordinary addition operator in JavaScript. It can perform both numeric addition and string concatenation depending on the types of the operands. TypeScript checks types at compile time but does not change the operator\'s behaviour.","The behaviour of the \\"+\\" operator after TypeScript is compiled\\nTypeScript is a layer on top of JavaScript, and after compilation all the code becomes ordinary JavaScript. The \\"+\\" operator in TypeScript is not overloaded and does not change its behaviour — it corresponds exactly to JavaScript\'s addition operator.\\n\\nHow the \\"+\\" operator works in JavaScript\\nIn JavaScript the \\"+\\" operator performs two functions: numeric addition and string concatenation. If at least one of the operands is a string, both operands are converted to strings and joined. If both operands are numbers, mathematical addition is performed.\\n\\nType checking at compile time\\nTypeScript checks that the types of the operands are compatible with the addition operation. For example, if you try to add a number and an object, TypeScript will report a compile error, even though in JavaScript it might work (with conversion to a string).\\n\\nConclusion\\nAfter TypeScript is compiled the \\"+\\" operator works exactly as it does in JavaScript. TypeScript merely adds static type checking at compile time, preventing potential errors caused by unexpected type conversion. This makes code more predictable and safer.","// Examples in TypeScript\\nlet a: number = 5;\\nlet b: number = 10;\\nlet result1 = a + b; // 15 (numeric addition)\\n\\nlet c: string = \\"Hello\\";\\nlet d: string = \\" World\\";\\nlet result2 = c + d; // \\"Hello World\\" (concatenation)\\n\\nlet mixed = a + c; // \\"5Hello\\" (the number is converted to a string)\\n\\n// An error in TypeScript\\nlet obj = { value: 10 };\\nlet num = 5;\\nlet result = num + obj; // Error: Object is possibly \'undefined\', or incompatible types"],[1967,"Sealed classes in Kotlin are classes that restrict the inheritance hierarchy, allowing subclasses to be created only within the same file. They are used to represent a fixed set of types — states or the results of operations, for example. This makes code safer and more predictable, especially when used in when expressions, where the compiler checks that every variant has been handled.","What are sealed classes?\\nSealed classes are a special kind of class in Kotlin that lets you create a restricted hierarchy of types. This means that all the possible subclasses of a sealed class are known at compile time and must be declared in the same file as the sealed class itself. That makes them ideal for modelling states, the results of operations or any other situation where the set of variants is fixed.\\n\\nHow does it work?\\nSealed classes are declared with the `sealed` keyword. Subclasses can be declared either inside the body of the sealed class or at the top level of the same file. The main advantage is that when a `when` expression is used the compiler checks that every possible variant has been handled, which rules out errors.\\n\\nWhere is it applied?\\nSealed classes are widely used in Android architecture (for representing UI states, for example), in functional programming (for algebraic data types) and in any scenario where you need to guarantee that every possible variant is handled. They are also useful when working with network requests, where the result may be a success, an error or still loading.\\n\\nConclusion\\nSealed classes are worth applying when you need to create a type with a fixed set of subclasses, in order to ensure type safety and avoid errors when handling all the possible variants. This makes code more readable and more maintainable.","sealed class Result {\\n    data class Success(val data: String) : Result()\\n    data class Error(val message: String) : Result()\\n    object Loading : Result()\\n}\\n\\nfun handleResult(result: Result) {\\n    when (result) {\\n        is Result.Success -> println(\\"Data: ${result.data}\\")\\n        is Result.Error -> println(\\"Error: ${result.message}\\")\\n        Result.Loading -> println(\\"Loading...\\")\\n    }\\n}"],[1968,"Access modifiers control the visibility of a class\'s fields and methods. Private makes a class member accessible only inside the class itself, hiding the implementation details. Protected opens access to the class and its descendants, letting them override the behaviour. This is the foundation of encapsulation, protecting data from incorrect use.","The purpose of access modifiers\\nIn object-oriented programming access modifiers (private, protected, public) determine which parts of the code can reach a class\'s fields and methods. They implement the principle of encapsulation — hiding an object\'s internal state and providing a controlled interface.\\n\\nPrivate\\nA field or method with the private modifier is accessible only inside the same class. This lets you hide implementation details that should not be changed from outside — an internal counter or temporary data, for example.\\n\\nHere balance is private, so external code cannot change it directly, only through the deposit and getBalance methods.\\n\\nProtected\\nProtected widens the access: the class\'s members are visible inside the class itself and in descendant classes. This is convenient when you need to let subclasses use or override behaviour while hiding it from the outside world.\\n\\nThe makeSound method is accessible in Dog but not from an instance of Animal or from external code.\\n\\nConclusion\\nPrivate and protected are key tools for building a reliable architecture. Private protects internal data from accidental change, while protected lets you extend classes flexibly through inheritance while keeping control of the interface. Using them is mandatory in any serious project in order to support encapsulation and reduce coupling in the code.","class BankAccount {\\n  private balance: number;\\n\\n  constructor(initial: number) {\\n    this.balance = initial;\\n  }\\n\\n  public deposit(amount: number): void {\\n    if (amount > 0) {\\n      this.balance += amount;\\n    }\\n  }\\n\\n  public getBalance(): number {\\n    return this.balance;\\n  }\\n}\\n\\nclass Animal {\\n  protected name: string;\\n\\n  constructor(name: string) {\\n    this.name = name;\\n  }\\n\\n  protected makeSound(): void {\\n    console.log(\'Some sound\');\\n  }\\n}\\n\\nclass Dog extends Animal {\\n  public bark(): void {\\n    this.makeSound(); // accessible, because it is protected\\n    console.log(\'Woof!\');\\n  }\\n}"],[1969,"To keep the UI consistent across projects people create a single component library or design system. The components live in a separate package (in a monorepo, for example) and are added as a dependency. This guarantees that every project uses the same interface elements, styles and behaviour.","What UI consistency is and why it is needed\\nUI consistency means that the user interface looks and behaves the same way in every part of an application or across a company\'s different projects. It improves the user experience, speeds up development and makes code easier to maintain.\\n\\nThe main approaches to maintaining consistency\\n\\n- A design system — a set of rules, tokens (colours, spacing, fonts) and components described in documentation.\\n- A component library — implemented UI components (buttons, inputs, modals) that can be reused.\\n- A monorepo — keeping the code of several projects and the shared library in one repository to make synchronization easier.\\n\\nAn example implementation in React\\n\\nHow this works in practice\\nThe design team creates mockups in Figma using the shared tokens. Developers implement the components in the library, which is published as an npm package or linked through a workspace in the monorepo. When the design changes, only the library is updated, and all the projects receive the changes once the dependency is updated.\\n\\nConclusion\\nUsing a design system and a component library is the standard way of maintaining UI consistency across several projects. It reduces code duplication, speeds up development and ensures a single user experience.","// packages/ui/src/Button.tsx\\nimport React from \'react\';\\nimport \'./Button.css\';\\n\\ninterface ButtonProps {\\n  variant: \'primary\' | \'secondary\';\\n  children: React.ReactNode;\\n}\\n\\nexport const Button: React.FC<ButtonProps> = ({ variant, children }) => {\\n  return <button className={`btn btn-${variant}`}>{children}</button>;\\n};\\n\\n// packages/app1/src/App.tsx\\nimport { Button } from \'@company/ui\';\\n\\nfunction App() {\\n  return <Button variant=\\"primary\\">Click me</Button>;\\n}"],[1970,"Interfaces let you separate the contract of a behaviour from its concrete implementation. This makes it possible to substitute implementations easily — for testing with mocks, for example, or for switching between different services. Instead of being tightly bound to a class, the code works through an abstraction, which makes the system flexible and extensible.","Why are interfaces needed when substituting implementations?\\nInterfaces in programming define a contract — the set of methods any suitable entity must implement. When code depends on an interface rather than on a concrete class, it becomes possible to substitute implementations without changing the code that uses them. This is the key principle of loose coupling.\\n\\nAn example in TypeScript\\n\\nWhere is this applied?\\n\\n- Testing: instead of a real database or API, mocks implementing the same interface are substituted.\\n- Modularity: you can switch between different providers (cloud storage services, for example) without changing the business logic.\\n- Dependency Injection: DI containers substitute the required implementations by interface automatically.\\n\\nConclusion\\nInterfaces are a tool for building a flexible architecture. Use them everywhere the implementation may change: in services, repositories and adapters. This makes code testable, extensible and independent of particular libraries or external systems.","interface ILogger {\\n  log(message: string): void;\\n}\\n\\nclass ConsoleLogger implements ILogger {\\n  log(message: string) {\\n    console.log(message);\\n  }\\n}\\n\\nclass FileLogger implements ILogger {\\n  log(message: string) {\\n    // write to a file\\n  }\\n}\\n\\nclass App {\\n  constructor(private logger: ILogger) {}\\n  run() {\\n    this.logger.log(\'App started\');\\n  }\\n}\\n\\n// Substituting the implementation\\nconst app = new App(new ConsoleLogger());\\n// or\\nconst app2 = new App(new FileLogger());"],[1971,"A model is not necessarily a class. A class is a template for creating objects in OOP, while a model is an abstract representation of data or business logic. A model can be implemented as a class, but it can also be a simple structure, an interface or even a function.","What are a model and a class?\\nIn programming the terms \\"model\\" and \\"class\\" are often confused, but they belong to different levels of abstraction. A class is a concrete construct in object-oriented languages (Java, Python, C++, for example) that defines the properties and methods for creating objects. A model is a broader notion describing the structure of an application\'s data or business logic, independently of the implementation language.\\n\\nExamples of the differences\\nConsider the model of a user in a web application. It can be implemented as a class:\\n\\nBut the same model can be represented by a simple object or by an interface in TypeScript:\\n\\nIn functional programming a model may be a function returning data, or even a JSON schema.\\n\\nWhere it is applied\\n\\n- In the MVC architecture the model is the layer responsible for data and business logic, which may include classes but is not limited to them.\\n- In databases a model describes the structure of tables and relations, which has no direct bearing on classes.\\n- In machine learning a model is a mathematical function trained on data, not a class in OOP.\\n\\nConclusion: a model is a conceptual representation of data or logic, while a class is one way of implementing it. Understanding this difference helps you choose the right tools for designing applications.","class User {\\n  constructor(name, email) {\\n    this.name = name;\\n    this.email = email;\\n  }\\n  getDisplayName() {\\n    return this.name;\\n  }\\n}\\n\\ninterface UserModel {\\n  name: string;\\n  email: string;\\n}"],[1972,"A generic lets you create a function that works with different types while preserving the typing. The type is stated in angle brackets before the parameters. At the call site TypeScript infers the concrete type automatically. This spares you from duplicating code and makes functions flexible.","What is a generic in a function?\\nA generic is a TypeScript mechanism that lets you create components which work with various types without losing type information. Instead of writing a separate function for every type, you use a type parameter that is substituted at the call site.\\n\\nThe syntax\\nThe type parameter is declared in angle brackets `<T>` before the function\'s parameter list. The letter `T` (for Type) is normally used, but any name is allowed.\\n\\nHow does it work?\\nWhen the function is called, TypeScript analyses the argument it was given and infers the concrete type for `T` automatically. In the example above `num` will have the type `number` and `str` the type `string`. If you need to state the type explicitly, you can write `identity<number>(42)`.\\n\\nA practical example: a function for working with arrays\\n\\nHere `T[]` means an array of elements of type `T`. The function returns the first element or `undefined`, preserving the type.\\n\\nGeneric constraints\\nSometimes you need to impose a constraint on the type in order to use particular properties. The `extends` keyword is applied for this:\\n\\nNow `T` can only be a type that has a `length` property.\\n\\nConclusion\\nGenerics make functions universal and type-safe. They are worth applying wherever a function has to work with different types while still preserving strict typing — in utilities for working with arrays, promises or HTTP requests, for example.","function identity<T>(arg: T): T {\\n  return arg;\\n}\\n\\nconst num = identity(42);      // T = number\\nconst str = identity(\'hello\'); // T = string\\n\\nfunction firstElement<T>(arr: T[]): T | undefined {\\n  return arr[0];\\n}\\n\\nconst firstNum = firstElement([1, 2, 3]);      // number | undefined\\nconst firstStr = firstElement([\'a\', \'b\']);     // string | undefined\\n\\nfunction getLength<T extends { length: number }>(arg: T): number {\\n  return arg.length;\\n}\\n\\ngetLength(\'hello\'); // 5\\ngetLength([1, 2]);  // 2\\ngetLength(42);      // Error: number has no length"],[1973,"void means that a function returns undefined or null — that is, it finishes normally but without a useful value. never means that the function never finishes (for example, it throws an exception or enters an infinite loop). void is the type for functions that complete without returning anything; never is for ones that do not complete.","The main difference between void and never\\nIn TypeScript both types are used to describe the return values of functions, but they denote fundamentally different situations. `void` indicates that the function finishes normally but does not return any useful value (in fact it returns `undefined`). `never`, by contrast, says that the function never finishes — either it throws an exception or it enters an infinite loop.\\n\\nExamples of use\\nConsider some simple examples:\\n\\nWhere they are applied\\n\\n- `void` is the standard type for functions that perform side effects (logging, changing the DOM, writing to a file) and do not return a value.\\n- `never` is useful for error-handling functions, for infinite loops (in game engines, for example) or for exhaustiveness checks on types.\\n\\nConclusion\\nUse `void` for ordinary functions that return nothing, and `never` when a function is guaranteed not to finish. This helps TypeScript analyse code more precisely and avoid errors related to unreachable execution paths.","function logMessage(msg: string): void {\\n  console.log(msg);\\n  // the function finishes but returns nothing\\n}\\n\\nfunction throwError(msg: string): never {\\n  throw new Error(msg);\\n  // after throw, execution stops\\n}\\n\\nfunction infiniteLoop(): never {\\n  while (true) {\\n    // an infinite loop, the function will never finish\\n  }\\n}"],[1974,"TypeScript is a static analyser that works only at compile time. All types, interfaces and annotations are removed when it is transpiled to JavaScript, so there is no typing at runtime at all. This means that type checks are not performed while the program runs, and type-related errors can only show up during operation.","TypeScript\'s limitations at runtime\\nTypeScript provides strict static typing, but it exists only at development and compile time. When the code is compiled to JavaScript, all the types are erased. This is the key limitation: TypeScript adds no runtime checks at all.\\n\\nAn example of type erasure\\n\\nAs you can see, the type annotations have been removed entirely. At runtime a string or an object can be passed in, and TypeScript cannot prevent it.\\n\\nThe practical consequences\\n\\n- There is no type checking at runtime — errors can only show up when the code runs.\\n- Types cannot be used to validate data from external sources (APIs, user input).\\n- There is no type reflection — you cannot obtain information about a type at runtime.\\n\\nHow to work around the limitations\\nFor runtime validation people use third-party libraries such as Zod or io-ts, which create schemas that work at runtime too. You can also use custom checks with typeof or instanceof.\\n\\nConclusion\\nTypeScript is useful for static analysis and for preventing errors at development time, but it does not replace runtime validation. For critical data from external sources, always use additional checks.","function add(a: number, b: number): number {\\n  return a + b;\\n}\\n\\n// After compilation to JavaScript:\\nfunction add(a, b) {\\n  return a + b;\\n}"],[1975,"TypeScript is a layer on top of JavaScript that adds static typing. During compilation, however, TypeScript is transpiled to JavaScript and all type annotations, interfaces included, are removed. JavaScript has no built-in type system, so checking the existence or structure of an interface at runtime is impossible.","Why do TypeScript interfaces not exist at runtime?\\nTypeScript is a statically typed language that compiles to JavaScript. During compilation TypeScript removes all type annotations, interfaces included, in a process called type erasure. JavaScript, which TypeScript compiles into, is a dynamically typed language and has no built-in support for checking types at runtime.\\n\\nAn example of type erasure\\nConsider a simple interface and a function:\\n\\nAfter compilation to JavaScript the code will look like this:\\n\\nAs you can see, the `User` interface has disappeared completely. JavaScript knows nothing of its existence.\\n\\nHow do you check an object\'s structure at runtime?\\nIf you need to check whether an object matches a particular structure at runtime, you can use:\\n- Custom type guards — functions that check the presence of properties and their types.\\n- Validation libraries such as `zod`, `io-ts` or `yup`, which let you define schemas and validate data at runtime.\\n\\nAn example with a custom guard\\n\\nThis code works because we check the properties\' actual types at runtime rather than relying on the interface.\\n\\nConclusion\\nTypeScript interfaces are a tool for the developer that helps catch errors at compile time. They are not meant for validating data at runtime. If you need runtime validation, use guard functions or specialized libraries.","interface User {\\n  name: string;\\n  age: number;\\n}\\n\\nfunction greet(user: User) {\\n  console.log(`Hello, ${user.name}!`);\\n}\\n\\nfunction greet(user) {\\n  console.log(`Hello, ${user.name}!`);\\n}\\n\\ninterface User {\\n  name: string;\\n  age: number;\\n}\\n\\nfunction isUser(obj: any): obj is User {\\n  return typeof obj.name === \'string\' && typeof obj.age === \'number\';\\n}\\n\\nconst data = { name: \'Alice\', age: 30 };\\nif (isUser(data)) {\\n  console.log(\'Data is a User\');\\n}"],[1976,"TypeScript provides no built-in runtime checks, because its type system works only at compile time. For checks while the code runs people use custom type guards, discriminated unions or libraries such as zod and io-ts. Type guards are functions that return a boolean and use the \'is\' keyword to narrow the type.","How does TypeScript handle types at runtime?\\nTypeScript is a layer on top of JavaScript that adds static typing. After compilation to JavaScript, however, all the type information is removed. This means TypeScript provides no built-in mechanisms for checking types while the program runs. Developers have to implement such checks themselves.\\n\\nCustom type guards\\nThe most widespread way is to create functions that check the structure of the data and return a boolean along with an indication of the type. Such functions are called type guards. They use the `is` keyword in their return type.\\n\\nDiscriminated unions\\nAnother approach is to use discriminated unions. This is when every object in the union has a shared property (the discriminant) with a unique literal type. TypeScript can narrow the type based on the value of that property.\\n\\nLibraries for runtime validation\\nFor more complex scenarios there are libraries such as `zod` and `io-ts`. They let you define data schemas and validate them at runtime, inferring the TypeScript types automatically.\\n\\nConclusion\\nRuntime checks in TypeScript are necessary for validating data that comes from external sources (APIs, user input). Use type guards for simple cases, discriminated unions for working with unions of types, and libraries like zod for complex schemas. This increases the reliability of an application and prevents errors caused by incorrect data.","interface Cat {\\n  meow(): void;\\n}\\ninterface Dog {\\n  bark(): void;\\n}\\n\\nfunction isCat(pet: Cat | Dog): pet is Cat {\\n  return (pet as Cat).meow !== undefined;\\n}\\n\\nfunction handlePet(pet: Cat | Dog) {\\n  if (isCat(pet)) {\\n    pet.meow(); // TypeScript knows that pet is a Cat\\n  } else {\\n    pet.bark(); // TypeScript knows that pet is a Dog\\n  }\\n}\\n\\ntype Shape =\\n  | { kind: \'circle\'; radius: number }\\n  | { kind: \'square\'; side: number };\\n\\nfunction area(shape: Shape): number {\\n  switch (shape.kind) {\\n    case \'circle\':\\n      return Math.PI * shape.radius ** 2;\\n    case \'square\':\\n      return shape.side ** 2;\\n  }\\n}\\n\\nimport { z } from \'zod\';\\n\\nconst UserSchema = z.object({\\n  name: z.string(),\\n  age: z.number().positive(),\\n});\\n\\ntype User = z.infer<typeof UserSchema>;\\n\\nconst data = JSON.parse(\'{\\"name\\":\\"Alice\\",\\"age\\":30}\');\\nconst user = UserSchema.parse(data); // will throw an error if the data is invalid"],[1977,"The entities that exist only at compile time in TypeScript include interfaces, type aliases, generics, decorators (in the old syntax) and type annotations. They are used for static checking of the code and generate no JavaScript after compilation. The exception is enums, which are partly compiled into objects, although their type annotations do disappear.","TypeScript entities that exist only at compile time\\nTypeScript is a layer on top of JavaScript that adds static typing. Many TypeScript constructs exist exclusively at compile time and have no representation at runtime. This means that after transpilation to JavaScript they are removed entirely or turned into ordinary JS code without types.\\n\\nThe main entities that disappear after compilation\\n\\n- Interfaces — used to describe the structure of objects, but they generate no code.\\n- Type aliases — like interfaces, they exist only for type checking.\\n- Type annotations — every type statement (`: string`, for example) is removed.\\n- Generics — the type parameters are erased, leaving only the logic without types.\\n- Decorators (in the old syntax) — although they can affect runtime, their type annotations disappear.\\n\\nA code example\\n\\nAs you can see, the `User` interface and the generic `<T>` have disappeared entirely. This lets TypeScript be fully compatible with JavaScript without adding any execution overhead.\\n\\nThe exception: enums\\nEnums are partly compiled into JavaScript objects, but their type annotations are removed as well. For example:\\n\\nSo the object itself exists at runtime, but the types associated with the enum disappear.\\n\\nConclusion\\nUnderstanding which TypeScript entities exist only at compile time helps developers avoid errors caused by trying to use types at runtime. This knowledge is especially useful when writing libraries, or when working with reflection where types may be needed dynamically.","// The original TypeScript\\ninterface User {\\n  name: string;\\n  age: number;\\n}\\n\\nfunction greet<T>(user: T): void {\\n  console.log(user);\\n}\\n\\n// After compilation to JavaScript\\nfunction greet(user) {\\n  console.log(user);\\n}\\n\\nenum Color { Red, Green, Blue }\\n// Compiles into:\\nvar Color;\\n(function (Color) {\\n  Color[Color[\\"Red\\"] = 0] = \\"Red\\";\\n  // ...\\n})(Color || (Color = {}));"],[1978,"High coupling between classes means the classes depend heavily on one another. This is dangerous because a change in one class often requires changes in others, which makes maintenance and testing harder. Such code becomes fragile and difficult to extend.","What is high coupling?\\nTight coupling arises when classes depend directly on the concrete implementations of other classes. For example, an `OrderService` class may create an instance of `MySQLDatabase` inside itself instead of receiving it through the constructor. This binds the code rigidly to one particular database.\\n\\nWhy is this dangerous?\\n\\n- Difficulty of change: replacing the database, moving from MySQL to PostgreSQL, would require rewriting every class that uses `MySQLDatabase` directly.\\n- Difficulty of testing: there is no way to substitute the real database with a mock in unit tests.\\n- Reduced reusability: the class cannot be used in another project without carrying all its dependencies over.\\n\\nAn example of the problem\\n\\nThe solution: dependency inversion\\nUse interfaces or abstractions so that classes depend on abstractions rather than on concrete implementations. Inject the dependencies through the constructor (dependency injection).\\n\\nConclusion: high coupling makes code fragile and hard to maintain. Apply the dependency inversion principle and dependency injection to create a flexible, testable architecture.","class MySQLDatabase {\\n  save(data) { /* ... */ }\\n}\\n\\nclass OrderService {\\n  constructor() {\\n    this.db = new MySQLDatabase(); // tight coupling\\n  }\\n  createOrder(order) {\\n    this.db.save(order);\\n  }\\n}\\n\\nclass OrderService {\\n  constructor(database) {\\n    this.db = database; // loose coupling\\n  }\\n  createOrder(order) {\\n    this.db.save(order);\\n  }\\n}\\n\\nconst db = new MySQLDatabase();\\nconst service = new OrderService(db);"],[1979,"Abstraction is the principle of hiding complex implementation details and exposing only the interface that is needed. It lets you work with objects at a high level without going into their internal workings. For example, when calling a `sendEmail()` method we do not think about the SMTP or TCP protocols.","What is abstraction?\\nAbstraction is one of the fundamental principles of object-oriented programming and of application design. It consists in singling out the key characteristics of an object or process and ignoring the inessential details. Put simply, it is a way of reducing complexity by presenting a system as a set of comprehensible interfaces.\\n\\nHow does it work in practice?\\nImagine you are using a television remote. You press the \\"volume up\\" button and the sound gets louder. You do not need to know exactly how the television processes the signal, which transistors are involved or how the digital-to-analogue converter works. The remote provides an abstraction — a simple interface for controlling a complex device.\\n\\nIn programming abstraction is implemented through classes, interfaces and abstract classes. For example:\\n\\nWhere is it applied?\\n\\n- In designing APIs and libraries — it hides internal complexity from developers.\\n- In application architecture (a layered architecture, for example) — each layer abstracts away the details of the layers below.\\n- In design patterns such as Factory, Strategy and Adapter.\\n\\nConclusion\\nAbstraction helps you manage complexity, making code more modular, testable and comprehensible. It is worth applying wherever you need to separate \\"what the system does\\" from \\"how it does it\\" — this is the basis for creating flexible, maintainable applications.","// The abstraction: an interface for sending notifications\\ninterface NotificationSender {\\n    void send(String message);\\n}\\n\\n// A concrete implementation for email\\nclass EmailSender implements NotificationSender {\\n    @Override\\n    public void send(String message) {\\n        // The complex logic of sending an email\\n        System.out.println(\\"Sending email: \\" + message);\\n    }\\n}\\n\\n// A concrete implementation for SMS\\nclass SMSSender implements NotificationSender {\\n    @Override\\n    public void send(String message) {\\n        // The complex logic of sending an SMS\\n        System.out.println(\\"Sending SMS: \\" + message);\\n    }\\n}\\n\\n// Using the abstraction\\npublic class NotificationService {\\n    private NotificationSender sender;\\n    \\n    public NotificationService(NotificationSender sender) {\\n        this.sender = sender;\\n    }\\n    \\n    public void notifyUser(String message) {\\n        sender.send(message); // We do not know exactly how it is sent\\n    }\\n}"],[1980,"The types of an application\'s entities are described with interfaces or type aliases in TypeScript. Interfaces are preferable for objects that may be extended, and type for unions and primitives. It is important to describe only the fields you need, avoiding redundancy, and to use optional properties (?) for data that is not required. This makes code self-documenting and prevents errors at compile time.","The basics of typing entities\\nWhen developing applications in TypeScript, describing the types of entities (a user, an order, a product, for example) correctly is key to ensuring type safety. It lets the compiler check that data is correct at development time, reducing the number of runtime errors. Types make code more readable and predictable, especially on large projects.\\n\\nInterfaces vs type aliases\\nEntities are most often described with interfaces or type aliases. Interfaces support declaration merging and inheritance, which is convenient for extending entities. Type aliases are a better fit for creating complex types such as unions or tuples. For example:\\n\\nPractical recommendations\\n\\n- Use interfaces for objects that may be extended in the future.\\n- Apply type for primitives, unions and types that do not need inheritance.\\n- Add optional fields (?) for data that may be absent.\\n- Avoid redundancy: do not duplicate types, use utilities such as Partial or Pick.\\n\\nAn example for an application entity\\n\\nConclusion: typing entities correctly increases the reliability of your code and makes it easier to maintain. Use interfaces for objects, type for simple or composite types, and always aim at minimalism when describing fields.","// An interface for a user\\ninterface User {\\n  id: number;\\n  name: string;\\n  email?: string; // an optional field\\n}\\n\\n// A type alias for an order\'s status\\ntype OrderStatus = \'pending\' | \'shipped\' | \'delivered\';\\n\\n// The product entity\\ninterface Product {\\n  id: string;\\n  title: string;\\n  price: number;\\n  description?: string;\\n  category: Category; // another interface\\n}\\n\\n// Use in a function\\nfunction getProductPrice(product: Product): number {\\n  return product.price;\\n}"],[1981,"The never type is inferred when a function never finishes (for example, it throws an exception or enters an infinite loop), or when every possible member of a union of types has been exhausted. It helps TypeScript guarantee that the code is correct and is used for exhaustiveness checks.","What is the never type in TypeScript?\\nThe `never` type represents values that can never occur. It is the bottom type in TypeScript\'s type hierarchy — that is, it can be assigned to any type, but nothing except `never` itself can be assigned to `never`. TypeScript infers `never` automatically in several key situations.\\n\\nWhen is never inferred?\\n\\n- Functions that never finish: if a function always throws an exception or enters an infinite loop, its return type is inferred as `never`.\\n- Exhaustive checks: when using discriminated unions and conditional constructs, once every possible variant has been handled the remaining branch gets the type `never`.\\n- Empty arrays with an explicit type: if an array is declared with the type `never[]`, it cannot contain elements.\\n\\nCode examples\\n\\nPractical use\\nThe `never` type is used actively to ensure type safety when working with unions of types. It helps a developer not to miss handling a new variant when the type is extended. `never` is also applied in conditional types to filter or exclude particular types from a union.\\n\\nConclusion\\nUnderstanding `never` is necessary for writing reliable TypeScript code, especially when working with complex types and discriminated unions. It is a powerful tool for static analysis that helps avoid errors at compile time.","// A function that throws an exception\\nfunction throwError(message: string): never {\\n  throw new Error(message);\\n}\\n\\n// An infinite loop\\nfunction infiniteLoop(): never {\\n  while (true) {}\\n}\\n\\n// An exhaustive check with a discriminated union\\ntype Shape = \\n  | { kind: \'circle\'; radius: number }\\n  | { kind: \'square\'; side: number };\\n\\nfunction area(shape: Shape): number {\\n  switch (shape.kind) {\\n    case \'circle\':\\n      return Math.PI * shape.radius ** 2;\\n    case \'square\':\\n      return shape.side ** 2;\\n    default:\\n      // shape has the type never here\\n      const _exhaustive: never = shape;\\n      return _exhaustive;\\n  }\\n}"],[1982,"String constants are better stored in a separate file or module, using objects or enums. This lets you avoid magic strings, makes changing values easier and prevents errors caused by typos. In JavaScript, for example, you can create an object with the constants and export it.","Why store string constants separately?\\nUsing string literals directly in code (so-called \\"magic strings\\") makes maintenance harder and increases the risk of errors. If the same string is used in several places, changing it would require a search and replace across the whole project. Moving the constants to a single place solves this problem.\\n\\nWays of storing them\\n\\n- An object with constants — a simple, clear way for JavaScript/TypeScript.\\n- An enum — suitable for TypeScript and other strictly typed languages.\\n- A separate file — the constants are grouped by meaning (api.js, messages.js, for example).\\n\\nAn example in JavaScript\\n\\nAn example in TypeScript with an enum\\n\\nConclusion\\nStoring string constants in a centralized place improves readability, makes refactoring easier and reduces the chance of errors. This approach is recommended in any project where strings are repeated or may change.","// constants/api.js\\nexport const API = {\\n  BASE_URL: \'https://api.example.com\',\\n  ENDPOINT_USERS: \'/users\',\\n  ENDPOINT_POSTS: \'/posts\'\\n};\\n\\n// Use\\nimport { API } from \'./constants/api\';\\nfetch(API.BASE_URL + API.ENDPOINT_USERS);\\n\\n// constants/status.ts\\nexport enum OrderStatus {\\n  PENDING = \'pending\',\\n  SHIPPED = \'shipped\',\\n  DELIVERED = \'delivered\'\\n}\\n\\n// Use\\nif (order.status === OrderStatus.SHIPPED) { ... }"],[1983,"Iterable is the most general type for objects you can iterate over (with a for loop, for example). Sequence is a subtype of Iterable that additionally supports access by index and has a fixed length. Iterable covers generators, sets and files, while Sequence covers lists, tuples and strings. Using Sequence in an annotation guarantees that the object passed in can not only be iterated over but also indexed.","The difference between Sequence and Iterable in Python type annotations\\nIn Python\'s type system (the typing module) Iterable and Sequence represent different levels of abstraction for collections. Iterable is the most general contract: an object implementing the iteration protocol (the __iter__() method, or __getitem__() with consecutive indices). Sequence is a stricter contract, inheriting from Iterable, Reversible and Collection, which guarantees support for access by index (__getitem__ with an arbitrary integer index), for determining the length (__len__) and for iterating in reverse.\\n\\nThe key differences\\n\\n- Iterable: can be used in a for loop and passed into functions that expect an iterator. Examples: lists, tuples, strings, sets, dictionaries, generators, files.\\n- Sequence: everything Iterable has, plus access by index (my_list[0], for example), slices and the .count() and .index() methods. Examples: list, tuple, str, range, bytes, bytearray.\\n\\nA code example\\n\\nWhen to use which\\nUse Iterable if your function only iterates over the elements and does not need access by index or the length. This makes the function more flexible — it will be able to accept generators and other lazy sequences. Use Sequence when you need access by index, slices or the guarantee that the data can be iterated over more than once (a generator can be iterated over only once).\\n\\nConclusion: Sequence is a stricter, richer type than Iterable. The choice between them depends on which operations you plan to perform on the collection. For maximum flexibility use Iterable; for a guarantee of access by index use Sequence.","from typing import Iterable, Sequence\\n\\ndef process_iterable(data: Iterable[int]) -> None:\\n    for item in data:\\n        print(item)\\n\\ndef process_sequence(data: Sequence[int]) -> None:\\n    # We can access it by index\\n    first = data[0]\\n    print(f\\"First element: {first}\\")\\n    # We can get the length\\n    print(f\\"Length: {len(data)}\\")\\n\\n# Examples of calls\\nprocess_iterable([1, 2, 3])  # OK\\nprocess_iterable((x for x in range(3)))  # OK, a generator\\nprocess_sequence([1, 2, 3])  # OK\\nprocess_sequence((1, 2, 3))  # OK, a tuple\\n# process_sequence((x for x in range(3)))  # Type error: a generator is not a Sequence"],[1984,"The reference variable for an object is stored on the stack, while the object itself lives on the heap. The stack is used to store primitive types and references to objects, because it is faster and has a strict order of memory allocation. The heap is intended for the dynamic allocation of memory for objects that may outlive the current function call.","Where variables and objects are stored in memory\\nIn programming languages such as Java, C# or JavaScript, memory is divided into two main areas: the stack and the heap. The stack is an area of memory that works on the LIFO (Last In, First Out) principle, where local variables and function calls are stored. The heap is an area for dynamic memory allocation, where objects and arrays are placed.\\n\\nHow this works in practice\\nWhen you create an object of a class — `new MyClass()`, for example — the object itself is placed on the heap. The variable that refers to that object is stored on the stack (if it is a local variable) or on the heap (if it is a field of another object). The variable contains not the object itself but the address (the reference) of the area of memory on the heap where the object lives.\\n\\nWhy this matters\\nUnderstanding this division helps you avoid errors related to memory leaks and incorrect management of references. For example, if a variable goes out of scope, the reference is removed from the stack, but the object on the heap may remain if other references to it exist. The garbage collector clears away such objects once no references to them are left.\\n\\nConclusion\\nThis division lets you manage memory efficiently: the stack provides fast access to local variables, while the heap allows objects with arbitrary lifetimes to be placed flexibly. Knowing this concept is necessary for writing performant, safe code.","public class Example {\\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\\n        MyClass obj = new MyClass(); // obj on the stack, the object on the heap\\n        int number = 42; // number on the stack\\n    }\\n}"],[1985,"Zod is a library for describing data schemas declaratively and validating against them. Unlike checking by hand, Zod generates TypeScript types automatically, provides strict typing and simplifies error handling. This reduces the amount of boilerplate code and increases reliability.","What is Zod and what is it for?\\nZod is a library for creating data schemas and validating against them, oriented towards TypeScript. It lets you describe the expected structure of your data (an object with name and age fields, for example) and then check whether a given value conforms to it. The main advantage is automatic type inference: a Zod schema produces a correct TypeScript type, which rules out discrepancies between the type declaration and the validation logic.\\n\\nThe differences from validating by hand\\nWhen validating by hand, a developer writes functions that check each field, handle errors and cast types manually. This leads to duplicated code and to errors. Zod, by contrast, offers a declarative approach: you describe the schema once, and the library takes care of checking, parsing and generating the types.\\n\\nA code example\\n\\nWhere it is applied\\nZod is often used to validate API input, forms, configuration and any external data where type safety matters. It is especially useful in TypeScript projects where runtime errors have to be minimized.\\n\\nConclusion: Zod simplifies validation and makes code more readable and safer thanks to automatic type generation. It is worth applying in any TypeScript project where data from unreliable sources has to be checked.","import { z } from \'zod\';\\n\\n// The schema\\nconst UserSchema = z.object({\\n  name: z.string().min(1, \'Name is required\'),\\n  age: z.number().int().positive(),\\n});\\n\\n// The automatic type\\ntype User = z.infer<typeof UserSchema>;\\n\\n// Validation\\nconst result = UserSchema.safeParse({ name: \'Alice\', age: 30 });\\nif (!result.success) {\\n  console.log(result.error.format());\\n} else {\\n  console.log(result.data); // strictly typed\\n}"],[1986,"If a plain as does not work, use a double cast through unknown: value as unknown as TargetType. This bypasses the type-compatibility check, because unknown is compatible with any type. Apply it only when you are sure of the data\'s structure, otherwise you risk a runtime error.","Forcing a type cast in TypeScript\\nTypeScript checks type compatibility strictly when the `as` operator is used. If you try to cast a type that has no overlap with the target type, the compiler reports an error. For example, you cannot cast a `string` to a `number` directly.\\n\\nThe solution through unknown\\nTo bypass this check, a double cast through `unknown` is used. First the value is cast to `unknown`, and then to the type you need. `unknown` is a supertype of all types, so such a cast is permitted.\\n\\nWhen this is useful\\n\\n- When working with data from external sources (an API, JSON) whose structure is known but which TypeScript cannot verify.\\n- When migrating code from JavaScript, while the types are not yet fully described.\\n- In rare cases where strict typing has to be bypassed for compatibility with libraries.\\n\\nThe risks and caveats\\nA double cast switches off type checking, which can lead to runtime errors. Use it only when you are absolutely sure of the data. It is better to apply custom type guards or runtime checks.\\n\\nConclusion: a double cast through `unknown` is a powerful but dangerous tool. Apply it only to bypass TypeScript\'s strict restrictions in controlled scenarios, and prefer safe type checks.","const value: string = \'123\';\\nconst num = value as unknown as number; // Works, but dangerous\\nconsole.log(num); // \'123\' (a string, not a number)\\n\\nfunction isNumber(value: unknown): value is number {\\n  return typeof value === \'number\';\\n}\\n\\nconst data: unknown = \'123\';\\nif (isNumber(data)) {\\n  console.log(data.toFixed(2)); // Safe\\n}"],[1987,"Yes, there are ESLint rules such as no-restricted-imports or import/no-internal-modules that can restrict imports from anything other than index files. This helps keep the architecture clean and avoid deep dependencies. For example, the import/no-internal-modules rule forbids importing from internal modules, allowing only index files.","What index files are and why use them\\nIndex files (usually index.js or index.ts) serve as a module\'s entry point, exporting only its public API. This hides the internal structure and makes refactoring easier.\\n\\nLinter rules for controlling imports\\nESLint with the eslint-plugin-import plugin provides several rules for restricting imports:\\n- import/no-internal-modules — forbids importing from a module\'s internal files, allowing only index files.\\n- no-restricted-imports — lets you specify a list of forbidden paths by hand.\\n\\nAn example configuration\\n\\nIn this example only imports from the index files inside the component folders are allowed.\\n\\nA practical example\\n\\nThe first import reaches the Button file directly; the second goes through the index file, which re-exports the component.\\n\\nConclusion\\nUsing linter rules to restrict imports from non-index files helps maintain a modular architecture, makes refactoring easier and makes code more predictable. This is especially useful in large projects with many modules.","// .eslintrc.js\\nmodule.exports = {\\n  plugins: [\'import\'],\\n  rules: {\\n    \'import/no-internal-modules\': [\'error\', {\\n      allow: [\'src/components/*/index\']\\n    }]\\n  }\\n};\\n\\n// Forbidden:\\nimport Button from \'./components/Button/Button\';\\n\\n// Allowed:\\nimport { Button } from \'./components/Button\';"],[1988,"Index files (usually index.js or index.ts) are used to group and re-export the modules of a folder. They simplify imports by letting you import everything you need from a single point rather than specifying the path to every file separately. This improves the readability of the code and reduces the number of import lines.","What are index files (barrel files)?\\nIndex files, often called barrel files, are files named index.js (or index.ts in TypeScript) that live in a folder and serve to re-export all the modules of that folder centrally. Instead of importing every component, utility or constant separately by its full path, you can import everything from a single index file.\\n\\nWhy are they needed?\\nThe main goal is to simplify the imports in a project and make them cleaner. Without index files, imports can look cumbersome, especially when the folder structure is deep. Barrel files also help hide the internal structure of folders by providing a single interface for external code.\\n\\nAn example of use\\nSuppose you have a components folder with several files:\\n\\nInside index.js you re-export everything:\\n\\nNow in another file you can import all the components in one line:\\n\\nInstead of writing three separate imports.\\n\\nWhen should they be applied?\\nBarrel files are especially useful in large projects with many modules, where they improve the readability and maintainability of the code. They should be used in moderation, however, since excessive use can lead to circular dependencies or slow the build down by importing unused modules. In small projects, or when there are strict performance requirements, you can do without them.","components/\\n  Button.js\\n  Input.js\\n  Modal.js\\n  index.js\\n\\n// components/index.js\\nexport { default as Button } from \'./Button\';\\nexport { default as Input } from \'./Input\';\\nexport { default as Modal } from \'./Modal\';\\n\\n// App.js\\nimport { Button, Input, Modal } from \'./components\';"],[1989,"ts-ignore is a comment that makes the TypeScript compiler ignore the next line. An explicit type cast (a type assertion) is a way of telling the compiler that you know the type better than it does. Both tools exist to bypass strict typing, but abusing them reduces the safety of your code. Use them only when you are sure of the type and TypeScript cannot infer it.","What are ts-ignore and a type assertion?\\n`ts-ignore` is the special comment `// @ts-ignore`, which switches off type checking for the next line. It is useful when you run into a type error caused by incompatible libraries or by temporary code. Using it often, however, can hide real problems.\\n\\nAn explicit type cast (a type assertion) is written with the syntax `as Type` or `<Type>`. It tells the compiler: \\"I know this value has this type, trust me.\\" This is safer than `ts-ignore`, since type checking still works for the rest of the code.\\n\\nExamples of use\\n\\nWhen to apply them\\n\\n- `ts-ignore` — only as a temporary solution, with a comment explaining why and a plan for fixing it.\\n- A type assertion — when you know the type for certain (after a runtime check, for example) and the compiler cannot infer it.\\n\\nConclusion: use these tools deliberately, preferring a type assertion over ts-ignore. They help you integrate JavaScript code or work with dynamic data, but they should not become a habit for bypassing the type system.","// ts-ignore: we ignore the error if the library has no types\\n// @ts-ignore\\nconst result = someLegacyLibrary.doSomething();\\n\\n// A type assertion: we are sure the element exists\\nconst input = document.getElementById(\'myInput\') as HTMLInputElement;\\ninput.value = \'Hello\';"],[1990,"TypeScript 5.5 brought several significant improvements. The main novelty is inferred type predicates, which improves type narrowing in functions. Control-flow analysis for constants has also been improved, support for isolatedDeclarations has been added for faster builds, and there are new capabilities for working with regular expressions.","The main novelties of TypeScript 5.5\\nTypeScript 5.5 is a release that significantly improves type inference and compilation performance. The key change is inferred type predicates, which lets TypeScript work out on its own when a function returns a type predicate, without the developer stating it explicitly.\\n\\nInferred Type Predicates\\nPreviously, to narrow types in a function you had to state the return type explicitly as a type predicate. Now TypeScript can infer this automatically:\\n\\nImproved control flow for constants\\nTypeScript 5.5 improved the analysis of constant expressions, which lets it determine types more precisely in complex conditions:\\n\\nIsolated Declarations\\nThe new isolatedDeclarations compiler option lets you generate .d.ts files faster by processing each file independently. This is especially useful for large projects and monorepos.\\n\\nImprovements for regular expressions\\nSupport for new flags has been added and the analysis of regular expressions improved, which helps avoid errors at compile time.\\n\\nConclusion: TypeScript 5.5 makes the language more convenient and more performant. Inferred type predicates simplify writing type-safe code, and the compilation-performance improvements matter for large projects. Following TypeScript releases is useful for every developer who works with the language.","function isString(value: unknown) {\\n  return typeof value === \\"string\\";\\n}\\n// TypeScript 5.5 infers automatically: (value: unknown) => value is string\\n\\nfunction process(value: string | number) {\\n  if (isString(value)) {\\n    console.log(value.toUpperCase()); // value: string\\n  }\\n}\\n\\nconst config = { mode: \\"development\\" as const };\\nif (config.mode === \\"development\\") {\\n  // TypeScript now understands that config.mode is \\"development\\"\\n}"],[1991,"The first member of a numeric enum gets the value 0 by default. If you do not state values explicitly, TypeScript assigns 0 to the first member automatically and gives every subsequent one the previous value plus 1. This is convenient for creating sequential constants without extra code.","How numeric enums work in TypeScript\\nNumeric enums in TypeScript are a way of defining a set of named constants with numeric values. If the values are not stated explicitly, the compiler assigns the value 0 to the first member automatically and gives every following one the previous value increased by 1. This behaviour is defined in the language specification and simplifies creating sequential enumerations.\\n\\nA code example\\n\\nIn this example Direction.Up gets the value 0 automatically, Down 1, Left 2 and Right 3. If you need to start from a different number, you can state the value for the first member explicitly, and the rest will continue the sequence.\\n\\nWhere this is applied\\n\\n- Defining states (order statuses, for example: New, Processing, Shipped).\\n- Creating flags or options with numeric identifiers.\\n- Simplifying code where the order of the members matters but the specific numbers do not.\\n\\nConclusion\\nUse numeric enums without explicit values when you need simple sequential numbering of constants. This makes code readable and reduces the risk of errors when new members are added.","enum Direction {\\n  Up,    // 0\\n  Down,  // 1\\n  Left,  // 2\\n  Right  // 3\\n}\\n\\nconsole.log(Direction.Up);    // 0\\nconsole.log(Direction.Down);  // 1"],[1992,"Yes, it can. Use generics with keyof to constrain the keys. For example, define a ColumnConfig type with the field key: keyof T. Then the function takes data of type T and an array of ColumnConfig. TypeScript will infer T automatically and check that key is only an existing key of the data.","Restricting the keys in a column configuration with TypeScript\\nTypeScript lets you type a column configuration strictly so that the `key` field can contain only the keys of the data that was passed in. This is achieved with generics and the `keyof` operator. The approach guarantees that you will not name a non-existent field, which prevents errors at compile time.\\n\\nHow it works\\nCreate a generic type `ColumnConfig`, where `T` is the data type. The `key` field must be `keyof T`. Then a function that takes the data and the column configuration uses the generic to infer the data\'s type.\\n\\nUse in practice\\nThis pattern is widely used in UI libraries (React Table, Ant Design Table, for example) to create type-safe tables. It is also useful when working with forms, where the form\'s fields have to match the keys of a data object.\\n\\nConclusion\\nUse generics with `keyof` to constrain the keys in a column configuration. This increases the type safety of your code, reduces the number of errors and improves autocompletion in the IDE. It is especially useful in large projects with many tables and forms.","type ColumnConfig<T> = {\\n  key: keyof T;\\n  title: string;\\n};\\n\\nfunction renderTable<T>(data: T[], columns: ColumnConfig<T>[]) {\\n  // the implementation\\n}\\n\\n// An example of use\\nconst users = [\\n  { id: 1, name: \'Alice\', age: 30 },\\n  { id: 2, name: \'Bob\', age: 25 }\\n];\\n\\nconst columns: ColumnConfig<typeof users[0]>[] = [\\n  { key: \'id\', title: \'ID\' },\\n  { key: \'name\', title: \'Name\' },\\n  // { key: \'email\', title: \'Email\' } // Error: \'email\' does not exist on the type\\n];\\n\\nrenderTable(users, columns);"],[1993,"Index types let you describe objects whose keys and values have particular types while the keys themselves are not known in advance. The Record utility creates an object type where every key from K has a value of type V. This is convenient for mappings, configurations and working with dynamic data, providing strict typing without losing flexibility.","What are index types and Record?\\nIn TypeScript index types let you describe objects whose keys may be arbitrary while the types of the keys and values are strictly defined. For example, the type `{ [key: string]: number }` means an object where all the keys are strings and the values are numbers. This is useful when the object\'s structure is not known in advance but the data types are fixed.\\n\\nThe Record utility\\nRecord is a built-in utility type that simplifies creating such types. Writing `Record<K, V>` is equivalent to `{ [P in K]: V }`. K may be a union of string literals or the type `string`, `number` or `symbol`. This makes code more readable and reduces duplication.\\n\\nExamples of use\\nConsider an example with an application\'s configuration:\\n\\nHere Record guarantees that the object contains only the given keys with the correct types. Another example is a mapping of statuses:\\n\\nA deeper understanding\\nIndex types and Record are closely related to the `keyof` operator, which extracts the union of a type\'s keys. For example, `keyof ConfigKeys` would give `\'apiUrl\' | \'timeout\' | \'retries\'`. This lets you create generic functions that work with any object while preserving the typing.\\n\\nRecord can also be nested: `Record<string, Record<string, V>>` for two-dimensional structures. It is worth remembering, however, that Record does not support optional keys — for that you need `Partial<Record<K, V>>`.\\n\\nConclusion\\nIndex types and Record are powerful tools for typing dynamic data structures. They are especially useful when working with configurations, mappings, API responses and any scenario where the keys are not fixed in advance but the types of the values are known. Using these constructs increases the reliability of your code and makes it easier to maintain.","type ConfigKeys = \'apiUrl\' | \'timeout\' | \'retries\';\\ntype Config = Record<ConfigKeys, string | number>;\\n\\nconst appConfig: Config = {\\n  apiUrl: \'https://api.example.com\',\\n  timeout: 5000,\\n  retries: 3\\n};\\n\\n// Error: \'debug\' is not part of ConfigKeys\\n// appConfig.debug = true;\\n\\ntype Status = \'active\' | \'inactive\' | \'pending\';\\ntype StatusMessages = Record<Status, string>;\\n\\nconst messages: StatusMessages = {\\n  active: \'User is active\',\\n  inactive: \'User is inactive\',\\n  pending: \'User is pending\'\\n};"],[1994,"The Interface Segregation Principle (ISP) states that clients should not depend on interfaces they do not use. It is better to have several specialized interfaces than one general one. This reduces coupling in the code and makes it easier to maintain. For example, instead of a single Printer interface with print, scan and fax methods, it is better to create a separate interface for each function.","What is the Interface Segregation Principle?\\nThe Interface Segregation Principle (ISP) is the fourth SOLID principle, formulated by Robert Martin. It states that no client should depend on methods it does not use. In other words, it is better to have several narrowly specialized interfaces than one large, universal one.\\n\\nWhy does this matter?\\nWhen an interface contains too many methods, the classes that implement it are forced to define even the methods they do not need. This leads to the following problems:\\n- Coupling in the code increases — changes to the interface affect every implementing class.\\n- Encapsulation is violated — clients gain access to functionality they do not require.\\n- The readability and maintainability of the code decline.\\n\\nAn example of violating ISP\\nImagine an interface for a multifunction device:\\n\\nIf a SimplePrinter class implements this interface, it will have to implement the scan and fax methods, which make no sense for a simple printer. This violates ISP.\\n\\nApplying ISP correctly\\nLet us split the interface into several specialized ones:\\n\\nNow the SimplePrinter class implements only the Printer interface, while a MultiFunctionPrinter class can implement all three. This makes the system more flexible and easier to extend.\\n\\nConclusion\\nThe Interface Segregation Principle helps you create loosely coupled, easily maintainable systems. It is worth applying when designing APIs, libraries and any abstractions where clients have different needs. By following ISP you reduce the number of unnecessary dependencies and make your code more resilient to change.","interface MultiFunctionPrinter {\\n    void print(Document d);\\n    void scan(Document d);\\n    void fax(Document d);\\n}\\n\\ninterface Printer {\\n    void print(Document d);\\n}\\ninterface Scanner {\\n    void scan(Document d);\\n}\\ninterface Fax {\\n    void fax(Document d);\\n}"],[1995,"Yes, a data type can be described as a set of permissible values. For example, the boolean type is the set {true, false}. The number type in TypeScript is the set of all numbers. This way of looking at it helps you define strictly which operations are allowed and which values a variable may hold.","The connection between data types and sets\\nIn mathematics a set is a collection of distinct elements. In programming a data type determines which values a variable may hold and which operations on them are allowed. A data type can therefore be regarded as the set of all the possible values of that type.\\n\\nExamples\\n\\n- The type `boolean` is the set `{true, false}`.\\n- The type `number` in TypeScript is the set of all numbers (floating-point ones).\\n- The type `string` is the set of all strings.\\n\\nPractical use\\nThis way of looking at it is useful when designing types and verifying the correctness of programs. For example, in TypeScript you can define a custom type as a union or an intersection of sets:\\n\\nConclusion\\nUnderstanding types as sets helps you think about data rigorously, avoid errors and design more reliable systems. It is especially useful when working with static typing and complex types.","type Status = \'active\' | \'inactive\'; // a set of two strings\\ntype Admin = User & { role: \'admin\' }; // an intersection of sets"],[1996,"When a function is declared with async, TypeScript wraps its return value in a Promise automatically. Even if the function returns a number or a string, the return type becomes Promise<number> or Promise<string>. This happens because async functions always return a Promise, which is what lets you use await when calling them.","How async changes the return type\\nThe `async` keyword in TypeScript wraps a function\'s return value in a Promise automatically. This is fundamental behaviour that guarantees async functions always return a Promise, even when there is no explicit return of a Promise inside.\\n\\nAn example\\n\\nWithout async the same function would simply return a number. With async TypeScript infers the type as Promise<number> automatically.\\n\\nWhy this matters\\n\\n- It ensures uniformity: all async functions return a Promise, which simplifies working with asynchronous code.\\n- It lets you use await when calling such functions.\\n- It prevents errors where a developer forgets to wrap the result in a Promise.\\n\\nConclusion\\nUse async when a function contains asynchronous operations (API requests, for example). This automatically makes the return type a Promise, which improves the readability and safety of the code.","async function getNumber(): Promise<number> {\\n  return 42;\\n}\\n\\n// The return type: Promise<number>\\nconst result = getNumber(); // result: Promise<number>"],[1997,"TypeScript inherited from JavaScript the ability to throw values of any type, primitives included. This is because TypeScript is a superset of JavaScript and does not restrict the behaviour of throw. In practice, however, it is recommended to throw only instances of the Error class, since they carry a call stack and make debugging easier.","Why does TypeScript let you throw values of any type?\\nTypeScript is a strict superset of JavaScript, which means any valid JavaScript code is also valid TypeScript code. In JavaScript the `throw` operator can be used with any value: strings, numbers, objects, functions and so on. TypeScript preserves this flexibility in order not to break compatibility with existing code.\\n\\nExamples of throwing different types\\n\\nWhy can this be a problem?\\nAlthough TypeScript lets you throw any value, this can complicate error handling. When using `catch` blocks the type of the caught value is `unknown` by default (from TypeScript 4.0), which requires an additional type check before use. If you throw only instances of `Error`, you can safely access the `message` and `stack` properties.\\n\\nRecommendations\\n\\n- Always throw instances of the `Error` class or its subclasses.\\n- Use custom error classes for more precise handling.\\n- Avoid throwing primitives, since this makes debugging harder.\\n\\nConclusion\\nTypeScript preserves the ability to throw any type for compatibility with JavaScript, but in practice it is recommended to use only instances of `Error` in order to ensure predictable error handling and better debugging.","throw \'An error\'; // a string\\nthrow 42; // a number\\nthrow { message: \'An error\' }; // an object\\nthrow new Error(\'An error\'); // an instance of Error"],[1998,"The JSON.parse and fetch methods return any because of backward compatibility and historical reasons. When TypeScript first appeared, unknown did not yet exist, and any was the only way to describe an unknown type. Changing the return type to unknown would break millions of existing projects. The TypeScript developers therefore left any in place, but recommend stating the type by hand with generics or annotations.","Why JSON.parse and fetch return any\\nIn TypeScript the `JSON.parse` and `fetch` methods return the type `any` rather than `unknown` for historical reasons. When TypeScript first appeared (in 2012) the `unknown` type did not yet exist — it was only added in version 3.0 (2018). At the time `any` was the only way to describe a value whose type is not known at compile time.\\n\\nThe backward-compatibility problem\\nChanging the return type from `any` to `unknown` would break millions of existing projects. The TypeScript developers decided to keep `any` for these methods so as not to break backward compatibility. They do, however, recommend that developers state the expected type by hand.\\n\\nHow to use them correctly\\nRather than relying on `any`, it is better to state the type explicitly:\\n\\nConclusion\\nUse explicit type annotations or generics when working with `JSON.parse` and `fetch` in order to preserve type safety. This will help you avoid runtime errors and make your code more predictable.","// Bad — returns any\\nconst data = JSON.parse(\'{\\"name\\":\\"John\\"}\');\\nconsole.log(data.age); // No error, but age may not exist\\n\\n// Good — we state the type\\ninterface User {\\n  name: string;\\n  age?: number;\\n}\\nconst user: User = JSON.parse(\'{\\"name\\":\\"John\\"}\');\\nconsole.log(user.age); // TypeScript will point out that age is optional\\n\\n// The same goes for fetch\\nconst response = await fetch(\'/api/user\');\\nconst userData: User = await response.json();"],[1999,"TypeScript has no equivalent of Java\'s throws because TypeScript follows JavaScript\'s philosophy, where exceptions are not part of the type system. In JavaScript errors can occur anywhere, and handling them with try/catch does not require a declaration in the signature. TypeScript focuses on the static typing of data rather than on controlling exceptions, which simplifies code and reduces coupling.","Why does TypeScript have no checked exceptions?\\nTypeScript, being a layer on top of JavaScript, inherits its dynamic nature of error handling. In JavaScript exceptions are not part of the type system — any function can throw any exception at any moment. TypeScript does not add checked exceptions (as in Java) because that would contradict the language\'s philosophy and complicate integration with existing JavaScript code.\\n\\nThe main reasons there is no throws in TypeScript\\n\\n- The dynamic nature of JavaScript: in JavaScript errors can arise not only explicitly (throw new Error()) but also implicitly (a ReferenceError when accessing a non-existent variable, for example). Declaring every possible exception in a signature is impossible.\\n- Compatibility with the ecosystem: TypeScript has to be backward compatible with JavaScript. Adding checked exceptions would require changing every existing library and piece of code.\\n- Simpler development: checked exceptions in Java often lead to cumbersome code with many try/catch blocks or throws clauses in signatures. TypeScript prefers a more concise approach.\\n\\nHow does TypeScript handle errors?\\nTypeScript uses return types to indicate possible errors explicitly. For example, you can return an object with an error field or use discriminated unions:\\n\\nThis approach makes error handling explicit and type-safe without requiring checked exceptions.\\n\\nConclusion\\nTypeScript does not include throws because of its dynamic foundation and its drive for simplicity. Instead it is recommended to use return types for explicit error handling, which makes code more predictable and easier to understand, especially on large projects.","type Result<T> = { success: true; data: T } | { success: false; error: string };\\n\\nfunction parseJSON(json: string): Result<any> {\\n  try {\\n    const data = JSON.parse(json);\\n    return { success: true, data };\\n  } catch (e) {\\n    return { success: false, error: (e as Error).message };\\n  }\\n}\\n\\nconst result = parseJSON(\'invalid\');\\nif (result.success) {\\n  console.log(result.data);\\n} else {\\n  console.error(result.error);\\n}"],[2000,"To create an interface from an existing one but without a particular field, use the built-in utility type Omit. Omit creates a new type by excluding the given keys from the source type. For example, `Omit<User, \'password\'>` will create a type without the password field. This lets you reuse existing types without duplicating them.","What is Omit and what is it for?\\nIn TypeScript a situation often arises where you need to create a new type from an existing one but exclude some fields. Instead of rewriting the whole interface by hand, you can use the built-in utility type `Omit`. It takes two parameters: the source type and a union of the keys that have to be excluded.\\n\\nAn example of use\\nSuppose we have a user interface with a password that must not be sent to the client:\\n\\nHow does it work under the hood?\\nThe `Omit` type is implemented as a combination of `Pick` and `Exclude`. It takes all the keys of the source type, excludes the given ones and then picks only the remaining fields. This lets you manipulate types flexibly without duplicating code.\\n\\nWhen is this useful?\\nOmit is especially useful when working with APIs, where sensitive data has to be hidden, or when creating partial versions of objects for edit forms. It helps you maintain type safety and avoid errors when the source interface changes.\\n\\nConclusion: use Omit to create derived types with fields excluded when you need to reuse an existing structure with restrictions. This improves the maintainability of your code and reduces duplication.","interface User {\\n  id: number;\\n  name: string;\\n  email: string;\\n  password: string;\\n}\\n\\n// We create a type without the password field\\ntype PublicUser = Omit<User, \'password\'>;\\n\\n// PublicUser now contains only id, name and email\\nconst user: PublicUser = {\\n  id: 1,\\n  name: \'Alice\',\\n  email: \'alice@example.com\'\\n};"]]');export{e as default};